var tafsirData_18 = {
  "1": "\"All praise is for Allah Who has revealed the Book to His servant, allowing no crookedness in it\" opens Surah al-Kahf with the same note of praise that closed the previous chapter. The chapter begins by praising the One who revealed the Quran to His servant Muhammad. This opening establishes the identity of the Book and the status of the one who received it.\n\nThe phrase \"His servant\" is an honor, as it was in the opening of Surah al-Isra. The Prophet is first and foremost a servant of God. His greatness lies not in being divine or having a share in divinity, but in his perfect devotion to the One who sent him. The same chapter that will later mention the stories of the cave, the two gardens, Moses and the servant of God, and the two great journeys of Dhu al-Qarnayn begins by affirming that Muhammad is a servant, not a god.\n\nThe description of the Book as having \"no crookedness\" is central. The word in Arabic, \"iwaj,\" means deviation, bend, or distortion. The Quran is straight, consistent, and free from contradictions. It does not twist with human desires, does not change over time, and does not contain hidden flaws. This straightness is one of its greatest signs.\n\nThe absence of crookedness includes several dimensions. The Quran's teachings are consistent with one another. Its accounts of earlier prophets agree with the truth in those stories. Its laws are balanced and free from extremes. Its arguments are sound. Its promises and warnings are reliable. A book without crookedness is a book that can be trusted.\n\nThis opening is a direct response to those who claimed the Prophet fabricated the Quran or that it was taught by a human being. A book produced over twenty-three years in varying circumstances, yet entirely free from internal contradiction, cannot be the product of a human author or multiple teachers.\n\nFor the reader, the first verse is an invitation to approach the Quran with confidence. It is not a book of confusion. It is a straight book from a straight God, revealed to a straight servant. The chapter that follows will demonstrate that straightness through stories that reveal the difference between truth and falsehood, faith and worldly attachment, knowledge and ignorance.\n\nPraising God at the beginning also teaches the reader that the proper response to revelation is gratitude. Before learning the details of the chapter, the heart is oriented toward the One who gave the Book. Worship precedes understanding.",
  "2": "\"making it perfectly upright, to warn the disbelievers of a severe torment from Him; to give good news to the believers—who do good—that they will have a fine reward\" continues the description of the Quran. The previous verse said it has no crookedness. This verse explains why it was made so straight.\n\nThe word \"perfectly upright\" reinforces the idea of straightness. The Quran is not merely free from error. It actively stands upright, establishing truth, justice, and balance in human life. It is \"qayyim,\" a word that suggests something that sets things right and keeps them in proper order.\n\nThe first purpose of this upright Book is to \"warn the disbelievers of a severe torment from Him.\" Warning is an act of mercy, not cruelty. A warning gives people the chance to change before it is too late. The Quran does not warn because God desires to punish. He warns because He desires that people turn back to Him.\n\nThe second purpose is to \"give good news to the believers—who do good—that they will have a fine reward.\" Good news is the counterpart of warning. The Quran does not only frighten. It also gives hope. The recipients of the good news are described as those who believe and do good, the same pairing that appears throughout the Quran. Faith and action go together.\n\nThe \"fine reward\" is described with a word that means something good, excellent, and beautiful. It includes Paradise, God's pleasure, and all the joys of the Hereafter that no eye has seen. The reward is fine because the One giving it is generous and the deeds that earn it are sincere.\n\nThe pairing of warning and good news in this verse sets the tone for the entire chapter. Surah al-Kahf contains stories of people who were saved because of faith and stories of those who were destroyed because of arrogance. It warns against attachment to the world and gives hope to those who remain patient upon truth.\n\nFor the reader, the verse clarifies the purpose of reading the Quran. It is not merely to gain information or to enjoy beautiful stories. It is to respond to the warning with repentance and to the good news with righteous action. The upright Book is meant to produce upright lives.",
  "3": "\"in which they will remain forever\" completes the description of the reward mentioned in the previous verse. The fine reward is not temporary. Those who receive it will remain in it forever.\n\nThe word \"forever\" translates \"abidan,\" which means eternal, without end. This is one of the essential beliefs of Islam about the Hereafter. Paradise is not a temporary place that people pass through. It is the final, eternal home of the believers.\n\nThe eternity of the reward makes it meaningful. If the joys of Paradise were to end, they would not be a perfect reward. The human heart longs for permanence, and only what is with God is permanent. The pleasures of this world fade, but the reward of the Hereafter does not.\n\nThis verse also implies that the torment warned of in the previous verse is eternal for those who earn it. The same chapter later describes the inhabitants of Paradise remaining in it forever, and the Quran in other passages describes the inhabitants of Hell remaining in it for as long as the heavens and earth endure, according to God's will. The precise nature of the Hereafter's duration is known to God, but the Quran makes clear that it is not like this worldly life, which comes to an end.\n\nFor the believers, the promise of eternity should transform how they live. The good deeds they do are not wasted effort. The difficulties they endure for God's sake are not permanent. Every act of worship, every moment of patience, every coin given in charity is being stored up for a life that never ends.\n\nFor those who reject the message, the verse is a reminder that the punishment they were warned about is also not a temporary inconvenience. The choice to disbelieve has eternal weight. The warning is given so that no one can say on the Day of Judgment that they were not told.\n\nFor the reader, this short verse is an invitation to measure every action against eternity. A life that leads to an endless reward is worth living with patience and gratitude. A life that leads away from that reward is a poor trade, no matter how pleasant it may seem in the short term.",
  "4": "\"and to warn those who claim, 'Allah has offspring.'\" turns from the promise of reward to a specific warning addressed to those who attribute children to God. The previous verses established the Quran as an upright Book of warning and good news. This verse identifies one of the most serious false beliefs it warns against.\n\nThe claim that God has offspring appears in various forms. The pagan Arabs claimed that the angels were daughters of God. Some Jews claimed that Ezra was the son of God. Some Christians claimed that Jesus was the son of God. The Quran rejects all such claims without distinguishing between them.\n\nAttributing offspring to God is not a minor theological error. It undermines the very foundation of monotheism. If God had a child, He would have a partner in His essence, and the distinction between Creator and creation would collapse. The Quran repeatedly argues that God is far above such things. He creates whatever He wills, but He does not beget, nor was He begotten.\n\nThe word \"warn\" is used again because this belief has severe consequences. To claim that God has offspring is to associate partners with Him, which is the one sin that Islam declares unforgivable if a person dies in it without repentance. It distorts the nature of God and leads to all forms of false worship.\n\nThis verse also sets up one of the themes of the chapter. Surah al-Kahf includes the story of Moses, the story of the people of the cave, and the figure of Dhu al-Qarnayn, all of which involve questions of power, knowledge, and the proper understanding of God's uniqueness. The warning about offspring is part of the chapter's broader defense of pure monotheism.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to purify belief about God. It is not enough to say that God exists. One must believe in Him correctly, without attributing to Him partners, children, or human limitations. The God who reveals the Quran is the one, self-sufficient Creator, and every other description of Him must be measured against that truth.",
  "5": "\"They have no knowledge of this, nor did their forefathers. What a terrible claim that comes out of their mouths! They say nothing but lies\" continues the condemnation of those who attribute offspring to God. The previous verse warned them. This verse exposes the basis of their claim.\n\nThe verse says they have no knowledge of what they claim. They have no evidence from revelation, no sound reasoning, and no genuine information. They are simply repeating a claim they inherited from previous generations without examining it. The word \"knowledge\" here is used deliberately. The claim about God having children is not based on knowledge. It is based on imitation and assumption.\n\nThe phrase \"nor did their forefathers\" traces the error to its source. People often believe things because their parents and ancestors believed them, not because they have examined the evidence. The Quran repeatedly challenges this kind of blind following. Truth is not determined by how many people believed it before you or how long ago they believed it.\n\nThe description of the claim as \"terrible\" expresses how deeply offensive it is to the truth. A claim about God that has no knowledge behind it is not merely a mistake. It is a grave falsehood. The word in Arabic suggests something enormous and monstrous.\n\nThe final clause is the strongest: \"They say nothing but lies.\" Every statement that attributes offspring to God is a lie, regardless of who says it or how sincerely they may believe it. Sincerity does not turn falsehood into truth. The person who worships a stone with a sincere heart is still worshipping a stone, and the person who claims God has a child is still making a false claim.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to examine religious beliefs. Do you believe things because you have examined them in light of the Quran and authentic teachings, or because they are what your family or culture has always said? The truth is not determined by ancestry. It is determined by knowledge.\n\nAt the same time, the verse should not be read as encouraging harshness toward people who hold such beliefs. The Quran itself debates with People of the Book in the best manner. The falsehood of the claim should be stated clearly, but the person making it should be invited to truth with wisdom and kindness.",
  "6": "\"Now, perhaps you O Prophet will grieve yourself to death over their denial, if they continue to disbelieve in this message\" addresses the Prophet directly after the condemnation of false beliefs. The previous verses described the disbelievers' claims. This verse turns to the emotional weight the Prophet carried because of them.\n\nThe phrase \"grieve yourself to death\" uses an Arabic expression that means to nearly destroy oneself with sorrow. The Prophet cared deeply about his people. He wanted them to be guided. When they rejected the message, mocked the truth, and attributed offspring to God, it pained him greatly.\n\nThe verse is a gentle consolation. God is telling the Prophet not to let his grief consume him. Their denial is not his failure. He has delivered the message clearly. Guidance is in God's hands, not in his. He is not responsible for the choices of people who refuse to believe.\n\nThe conditional phrase \"if they continue to disbelieve\" acknowledges that the Prophet's grief would increase as their rejection continued. The more he cared, the more their stubbornness hurt. God is not rebuking him for caring. He is reassuring him that there is a limit to what any messenger can do.\n\nThis verse has comforted every caller to Islam since the Prophet. When you care about people and they reject the truth, you can feel responsible, as if their disbelief is your fault. But the Quran teaches that the messenger's duty is to deliver. He cannot open hearts that have chosen to remain closed.\n\nThe verse also reveals the humanity of the Prophet. He was not a robot delivering a message without feeling. He grieved. He hoped. He was pained by the rejection of his people. His love for them was so real that God had to console him and tell him not to grieve himself to death.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder to balance effort with trust. Do your best to share the truth, live by it, and call people to it with wisdom. But do not destroy yourself when some refuse. The outcome belongs to God, and your responsibility is to be faithful, not to force faith into unwilling hearts.",
  "7": "\"We have indeed made whatever is on earth as an adornment for it, in order to test which of them is best in deeds\" explains why the world appears attractive and why people are tested through it. The previous verse consoled the Prophet about disbelief. This verse reveals the larger purpose behind the world's adornment.\n\nThe phrase \"whatever is on earth\" includes everything that people find beautiful and desirable: wealth, children, landscapes, animals, food, status, and power. God did not create the earth barren and ugly. He adorned it. The beauty of the world is a sign of His generosity and artistry.\n\nBut this adornment is not merely for enjoyment. It is a test. God gave people beautiful things to see how they would respond. Would they use them in gratitude and obedience, or would they become attached to them and forget the Giver? The adornment reveals what is in the heart.\n\nThe phrase \"which of them is best in deeds\" is important. The test is not who has the most. It is who does the best. Quality matters more than quantity. A small deed done with sincerity and in accordance with the Prophet's way is greater than a large deed done for show. The best deeds are those that are done purely for God and correctly according to His guidance.\n\nThe verse also provides perspective on the worldly attractions that cause people to reject faith. The disbelievers of Mecca were attached to their wealth, status, and traditions. The Quran says these things are adornment, not reality. They are temporary decorations on a temporary earth. The real test is what a person does with them.\n\nFor the reader, the verse transforms how you see the world. Every beautiful thing is both a gift and a test. The money you earn, the family you love, the talents you have, and the beauty you enjoy are meant to bring you closer to God, not to distract you from Him. The wise person enjoys the adornment of the earth but keeps their heart attached to the One who adorned it.",
  "8": "\"And We will certainly reduce whatever is on it to barren ground\" contrasts the temporary adornment of the earth with its final end. The previous verse said that everything on earth is an adornment for testing. This verse says that all of it will eventually be stripped away.\n\nThe phrase \"barren ground\" means dry, bare, without vegetation or life. On the Day of Judgment, the mountains will be moved, the earth will be leveled, and everything that once made it beautiful will vanish. The gardens will become dust. The palaces will be ruins. The wealth will be gone. The adornment was temporary by design.\n\nThis verse is a natural continuation of the previous one. Once you understand that the world is an adornment for testing, you understand that it cannot last. A test has a beginning and an end. The test of this life ends when this life ends. What remains is the record of deeds and the eternal home.\n\nThe word \"certainly\" leaves no doubt. The destruction of the world's adornment is not a possibility. It is a certainty. The One who created the earth and adorned it has promised to reduce it to barren ground, and His promise never fails.\n\nThis does not mean that the earth is evil or that believers should despise it. The Quran teaches people to enjoy the good things God has provided. But it means that the earth is not the final home. A person who builds their life entirely on its adornment is building on sand.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to balance. Enjoy what God has given you, but do not be deceived by it. Use the adornment of the world to do good, to give in charity, to support your family, and to worship God. But do not let it own your heart. The ground that is now covered with beauty will one day be bare, and only your deeds will remain.\n\nThe pairing of verses 7 and 8 is one of the most concise descriptions of worldly life in the Quran: adorned for testing, then reduced to barren ground. The person who remembers both truths lives in this world without being imprisoned by it.",
  "9": "“Have you ˹O Prophet˺ thought that the people of the cave and the plaque were ˹the only˺ wonders of Our signs?”” opens the story of the cave, and the commentators note the two parts of the question. The verse asks the question: “Have you O Prophet thought that the people of the cave and the plaque were the only wonders of Our signs?” or “am hasibta anna ashaba al-kahfi war-raqeemi kanoo min ayatina ‘ajaban,” and the commentators note the question, for the verse corrects the assumption that the story of the cave is the greatest of the wonders, and the question is the introduction, the declaration that the signs of the Lord are the many, and the story of the cave is the one of them, and the commentators explain the “raqeem,” the plaque, the inscription, and the reports differ on its meaning, and the verse is the record of the question, the introduction of the story that the following verses unfold. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the cave is the refuge, and the plaque is the inscription, and the verse is the record of the question, the introduction that corrects the measure of the wonder, and the wonder of the cave is the sign that the believer reflects upon, and the signs of the Lord are the greater.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reflection. The believer is taught that the signs of the Lord are the many, and the story of the cave is the one, and the believer who reflects upon the story is the believer whose faith the sign strengthens, and the question of the verse is the invitation, the call to the reflection upon the wonder, and the believer who reads the story with the reflection is the believer whose heart the sign softens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the measure of the sign: the cave is the wonder, and the signs of the Lord are the greater, and the believer who reflects is the believer who learns. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the reflection that the story invites.",
  "10": "“˹Remember˺ when those youths took refuge in the cave, and said, “Our Lord! Grant us mercy from Yourself and guide us rightly through our ordeal.”” records the refuge and the prayer, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the refuge: “when those youths took refuge in the cave,” or “ith awa al-fityatu ila al-kahfi,” and the commentators note the refuge, for the youths fled to the cave, and the refuge is the flight of the faith, the retreat of the believers from the persecution of the idolatrous people, and the commentators note the detail, for the youths were the believers who fled the king, and the cave is the refuge that the Lord prepared. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the refuge is the flight, and the flight is the faith, and the verse is the record of the refuge, the retreat of the believers. The second part is the prayer: “Our Lord! Grant us mercy from Yourself and guide us rightly through our ordeal,” or “rabbana atina min ladunka rahmatan wa hayyi’ lana min amrina rashadan,” and the commentators note the prayer, for the youths asked the mercy and the guidance, and the prayer is the reliance, the turning of the believers to the Lord, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the reliance that the following verse answers with the sleep, and the commentators note the significance, for the prayer is the model of the believer's supplication, the seeking of the mercy and the guidance.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the refuge, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own flight. The believer is taught that the flight of the faith is the refuge, and the one who flees the persecution is the one whom the Lord shelters, and the believer who seeks the mercy and the guidance is the believer whose prayer the Lord answers, and the prayer of the youths is the model, the supplication of the believer in the trial, and the believer who flees with the faith is the believer whose refuge the Lord prepares. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the refuge of the faith: the youths fled and prayed, and the believer who flees the sin is the believer whom the Lord shelters, and the prayer is the key of the refuge. The verse is the record of the refuge and the prayer, and the teaching of the flight that the faith makes.",
  "11": "“So We caused them to fall into a dead sleep in the cave for many years,” records the sleep, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the sleep: “So We caused them to fall into a dead sleep in the cave for many years,” or “fa-darabna ‘ala athanihim fi al-kahfi sineena ‘adadan,” and the commentators note the phrase “darabna ‘ala athanihim” — We struck upon their ears — for the sleep was sealed upon the ears, and the sealing is the depth of the sleep, and the “sineen ‘adadan” — the numbered years — is the length, and the verse is the record of the sleep, the miracle that the Lord granted, and the commentators note the significance, for the sleep is the protection, the concealment of the youths from the persecution, and the verse is the record of the sleep, the mercy that the following verse complements. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the sleep is the miracle, and the miracle is the protection, and the verse is the record of the sleep, the concealment that the Lord granted.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sleep, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the protection of the Lord is the thing that the believer cannot secure, and the one whom the Lord shelters is the one whom the persecution cannot reach, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose protection the Lord grants, and the sleep of the youths is the teaching, the declaration that the Lord guards the faithful, and the believer who reads the sleep is the believer whose trust the miracle strengthens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the protection of the Lord: the youths slept and were hidden, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whom the Lord conceals, and the sleep is the mercy that the believer cannot earn. The verse is the record of the sleep, and the teaching of the protection that the Lord supplies.",
  "12": "“then We raised them so We may show which of the two groups would make a better estimation of the length of their stay.”” records the awakening, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the raising: “then We raised them,” or “thumma ba‘athnahum,” and the commentators note the raising, for the Lord awakened the youths, and the awakening is the second stage, and the verse is the record of the raising, the step that the following verse describes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the raising is the awakening, and the awakening is the sign, and the verse is the record of the raising, the step of the demonstration. The second part is the purpose: “so We may show which of the two groups would make a better estimation of the length of their stay,” or “li-na‘lama ayyu al-hizbayni ahsa li-ma labithoo amadan,” and the commentators note the purpose, for the raising was to show which group would estimate the length of the stay, and the purpose is the demonstration, the teaching that the knowledge of the length is the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the purpose, the teaching that the following verses develop.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the awakening, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the awakening of the youths is the sign of the resurrection, and the one who reflects upon the sleep and the raising is the one whose faith the sign strengthens, and the believer who trusts the power of the Lord is the believer whose certainty the demonstration deepens, and the raising of the youths is the proof, the declaration that the Lord raises the dead, and the believer who reads the raising is the believer whose hope the resurrection sustains. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the proof of the resurrection: the youths were raised, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose certainty the sign confirms, and the raising is the teaching that the power of the Lord is the complete. The verse is the record of the raising, and the teaching of the certainty that the awakening produces.",
  "13": "“We relate to you ˹O Prophet˺ their story in truth. They were youths who truly believed in their Lord, and We increased them in guidance.”” records the introduction, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the declaration: “We relate to you O Prophet their story in truth,” or “nahnu naqussu ‘alayka naba’ahum bil-haqqi,” and the commentators note the declaration, for the Lord related the story in the truth, and the declaration is the assurance, the statement that the story is the true account, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the introduction of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the truth is the assurance, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the introduction of the account. The second part is the description: “They were youths who truly believed in their Lord, and We increased them in guidance,” or “innahum fityatun amanoo bi-rabbihim wa zidnahum hudan,” and the commentators note the description, for the youths were the believers, and the Lord increased them in the guidance, and the description is the praise, the declaration that the faith of the youths was the sincere and the guidance was the gift, and the verse is the record of the description, the praise that the following verses develop.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the guidance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own faith. The believer is taught that the faith is the foundation, and the guidance is the gift, and the one who believes is the one whom the Lord increases, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose guidance the Lord grows, and the description of the youths is the model, the declaration that the faith of the young is the sincere, and the believer who reads the description is the believer whose hope the increase sustains. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the faith: the youths believed and were increased, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whom the Lord guides, and the increase is the promise that the believer seeks. The verse is the record of the introduction, and the teaching of the guidance that the faith earns.",
  "14": "“And We strengthened their hearts when they stood up and declared, “Our Lord is the Lord of the heavens and the earth. We will never call upon any god besides Him, or we would truly be uttering an outrageous lie.”” records the declaration, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the strengthening: “And We strengthened their hearts when they stood up and declared,” or “wa rabatna ‘ala quloobihim ith qamoo fa-qaloo,” and the commentators note the strengthening, for the Lord bound the hearts of the youths, and the strengthening is the gift, the courage that the declaration required, and the verse is the record of the strengthening, the gift that the faith received. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the strengthening is the courage, and the courage is the gift, and the verse is the record of the strengthening, the gift that the declaration displayed. The second part is the declaration: “Our Lord is the Lord of the heavens and the earth. We will never call upon any god besides Him, or we would truly be uttering an outrageous lie,” or “rabbuna rabbu as-samawati wal-ardi lan nad‘uwa min doonihi ilahan laqad qulna ithan shatatan,” and the commentators note the declaration, for the youths declared the oneness, and the declaration is the faith, the public statement of the monotheism, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the faith that the following verses describe.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the declaration, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own courage. The believer is taught that the declaration of the truth is the courage, and the one who stands for the oneness is the one whose heart the Lord strengthens, and the believer who declares the faith is the believer whose declaration the Lord values, and the declaration of the youths is the model, the public statement of the monotheism, and the believer who reads the declaration is the believer whose courage the model strengthens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the courage of the declaration: the youths declared and were strengthened, and the believer who stands for the truth is the believer whom the Lord supports, and the declaration is the faith that the believer must speak. The verse is the record of the declaration, and the teaching of the courage that the truth demands.",
  "15": "“˹Then they said to one another,˺ “These people of ours have taken gods besides Him. Why do they not produce a clear proof of them? Who then does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah?”” records the argument of the youths, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the observation: “These people of ours have taken gods besides Him. Why do they not produce a clear proof of them?” or “ha’ula’i qawmuna ittakhathoo min doonihi alihatan law la ya’toona ‘alayhim bi-sultanin bayyin,” and the commentators note the observation, for the youths observed that their people had taken the gods without the proof, and the observation is the argument, the demand of the evidence, and the verse is the record of the observation, the argument that the falsehood could not answer. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the observation is the argument, and the argument is the truth, and the verse is the record of the observation, the demand of the proof. The second part is the question: “Who then does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah?” or “fa-man azlamu mimmani iftara ‘ala Allahi kathiban,” and the commentators note the question, for the youths declared that the fabrication of the lie against the Lord is the greatest of the wrongs, and the question is the verdict, the declaration that the idolatry is the wrong, and the verse is the record of the question, the verdict that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the argument, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reasoning. The believer is taught that the demand of the proof is the argument of the truth, and the one who worships without the proof is the one whose worship the reason refutes, and the believer who reasons about the faith is the believer whose faith the reason anchors, and the question of the youths is the model, the argument that the falsehood cannot answer, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose discernment the argument sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reasoning of the faith: the youths demanded the proof, and the believer who reasons is the believer whose faith the reason confirms, and the fabrication of the lie is the wrong that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the argument, and the teaching of the reasoning that the faith requires.",
  "16": "“Since you have distanced yourselves from them and what they worship besides Allah, take refuge in the cave. Your Lord will extend His mercy to you and accommodate you in your ordeal.”” records the counsel, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the command: “Since you have distanced yourselves from them and what they worship besides Allah, take refuge in the cave,” or “wa ithi i‘tazaltumoohum wa ma ya‘budoona illa Allaha fa’woo ila al-kahfi,” and the commentators note the command, for the youths counselled one another to take refuge in the cave after the separation, and the command is the plan, the retreat of the believers, and the verse is the record of the command, the step that the following verses fulfill. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the separation is the departure, and the cave is the refuge, and the verse is the record of the command, the plan of the flight. The second part is the promise: “Your Lord will extend His mercy to you and accommodate you in your ordeal,” or “yanshur lakum rabbukum min rahmatihi wa yuhayyi’ lakum min amrikum mirfaqan,” and the commentators note the promise, for the youths promised one another the mercy of the Lord, and the promise is the trust, the reliance upon the Lord, and the verse is the record of the promise, the trust that the following verses confirm.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the separation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own retreat. The believer is taught that the separation from the falsehood is the first step of the refuge, and the one who distances from the sin is the one whom the Lord shelters, and the believer who retreats for the faith is the believer whose refuge the Lord prepares, and the promise of the mercy is the trust, the reliance that the believer holds, and the believer who reads the counsel is the believer whose retreat the Lord guides. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the wisdom of the separation: the youths distanced and sheltered, and the believer who separates from the sin is the believer whom the Lord protects, and the mercy is the promise that the believer trusts. The verse is the record of the counsel, and the teaching of the separation that the refuge requires.",
  "17": "“And you would have seen the sun, as it rose, inclining away from their cave to the right, and as it set, declining away from them to the left, while they lay in its open space. That is one of the signs of Allah. Whoever Allah guides is truly guided. But whoever He leaves to stray, you will never find for them a guiding mentor.”” records the sign of the sun, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the sign: “you would have seen the sun, as it rose, inclining away from their cave to the right, and as it set, declining away from them to the left, while they lay in its open space,” or “wa tara ash-shamsa itha tala‘at tazawaru ‘an kahfihim thata al-yameeni wa itha gharabat taqriduhum thata ash-shimali wa hum fee fajwatin minhu,” and the commentators note the sign, for the sun inclined away from the cave at the rising and the setting, and the inclination is the miracle, the protection of the youths from the heat, and the verse is the record of the sign, the care that the Lord displayed. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the inclination of the sun is the protection, and the protection is the care, and the verse is the record of the sign, the care that the following clause reflects upon. The second part is the principle: “That is one of the signs of Allah. Whoever Allah guides is truly guided. But whoever He leaves to stray, you will never find for them a guiding mentor,” or “thalika min ayati Allahi man yahdi Allahu fa-huwa al-muhtadi wa man yudlil fa-lan tajida lahu waliyyan murshidan,” and the commentators note the principle, for the guidance and the misguidance are the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the principle, the teaching that the guidance is the gift.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own care. The believer is taught that the care of the Lord is the thing that the believer cannot secure, and the one whom the Lord protects is the one whom the elements serve, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose care the Lord arranges, and the sign of the sun is the teaching, the declaration that the Lord arranges the details, and the believer who reads the sign is the believer whose trust the care strengthens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the care of the Lord: the sun inclined, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose affairs the Lord arranges, and the guidance is the gift that the believer seeks. The verse is the record of the sign, and the teaching of the care that the Lord supplies.",
  "18": "“And you would have thought they were awake, though they were asleep. We turned them over, to the right and left, while their dog stretched his forelegs at the entrance. Had you looked at them, you would have certainly fled away from them, filled with horror.”” records the state of the sleepers, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the state: “you would have thought they were awake, though they were asleep. We turned them over, to the right and left, while their dog stretched his forelegs at the entrance,” or “wa tahsabuhum ayqazan wa hum ruqoodun wa nuqallibuhum thata al-yameeni wa thata ash-shimali wa kalbuhum basitun thira‘ayhi bil-waseed,” and the commentators note the state, for the youths appeared awake while they slept, and the turning of the bodies is the care, the preservation of the flesh, and the dog is the companion, the guardian at the entrance, and the verse is the record of the state, the care that the Lord displayed. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the turning is the preservation, and the dog is the guard, and the verse is the record of the state, the care that the following clause completes. The second part is the horror: “Had you looked at them, you would have certainly fled away from them, filled with horror,” or “lawi ittala‘ta ‘alayhim la-wallayta minhum firaran wa la-muli’ta minhum ru‘ban,” and the commentators note the horror, for the sight of the sleepers was the awe, and the verse is the record of the horror, the description of the majesty that the sign carried.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the care, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the care of the Lord is the thing that the believer cannot secure, and the preservation of the bodies is the gift, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose preservation the Lord arranges, and the horror of the sight is the teaching, the declaration that the sign of the Lord is the awe, and the believer who reads the description is the believer whose trust the care strengthens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the preservation of the Lord: the bodies were turned, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whom the Lord preserves, and the horror is the measure of the sign. The verse is the record of the state, and the teaching of the care that the Lord displays.",
  "19": "“And so We awakened them so that they might question one another. One of them exclaimed, “How long have you remained ˹asleep˺?” Some replied, “Perhaps a day, or part of a day.” They said ˹to one another˺, “Your Lord knows best how long you have remained. So send one of you with these silver coins of yours to the city, and let him find which food is the purest, and then bring you provisions from it. Let him be ˹exceptionally˺ cautious, and do not let him give you away.”” records the awakening and the exchange, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the exchange: “We awakened them so that they might question one another. One of them exclaimed, ‘How long have you remained asleep?’ Some replied, ‘Perhaps a day, or part of a day,’” or “wa kathalika ba‘athnahum li-yatasa’aloo baynahum qala qa’ilun minhum kam labithtum qaloo labithna yawman aw ba‘da yawmin,” and the commentators note the exchange, for the youths questioned the length of the stay, and the estimate is the human, and the verse is the record of the exchange, the wonder that the following clause resolves. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the exchange is the awakening, and the awakening is the sign, and the verse is the record of the exchange, the wonder of the time. The second part is the instruction: “Your Lord knows best how long you have remained. So send one of you with these silver coins of yours to the city… and do not let him give you away,” or “qaloo rabbukum a‘lamu bima labithtum fab‘athoo ahadakum bi-wariqikum hathihi ila al-madeenati… wa la yush‘iranna bikum ahadan,” and the commentators note the instruction, for the youths sent the one with the coins, and the instruction is the prudence, the caution, and the verse is the record of the instruction, the prudence that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prudence, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own caution. The believer is taught that the prudence is the duty, and the one who acts with the caution is the one whose affair the Lord guides, and the believer who plans with the prudence is the believer whose plan the Lord blesses, and the instruction of the youths is the model, the prudence that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the instruction is the believer whose caution the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prudence of the believer: the youths planned and cautioned, and the believer who plans is the believer whose plan the Lord guides, and the caution is the duty that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the awakening, and the teaching of the prudence that the believer exercises.",
  "20": "“For, indeed, if they find out about you, they will stone you ˹to death˺, or force you back into their faith, and then you will never succeed.”” records the warning, and the commentators note the two parts of the warning. The first is the danger: “For, indeed, if they find out about you, they will stone you to death, or force you back into their faith,” or “innahum in yazharoo ‘alaykum yarjumookum aw yu‘eedookum fee millatihim,” and the commentators note the danger, for the youths warned one another of the stoning and the forcing, and the warning is the measure of the persecution, and the verse is the record of the warning, the danger that the prudence of the previous verse had anticipated. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the stoning is the punishment, and the forcing is the apostasy, and the verse is the record of the warning, the danger of the discovery. The second part is the consequence: “and then you will never succeed,” or “wa lan tuflihoo ithan abadan,” and the commentators note the consequence, for the return to the faith of the people is the loss, and the verse is the record of the warning, the teaching that the apostasy is the failure, and the commentators note the significance, for the verse declares that the success is the holding of the faith, and the loss is the surrender of it.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the warning, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own steadfastness. The believer is taught that the holding of the faith is the success, and the surrender of it is the failure, and the one who is threatened is the one whose steadfastness the Lord values, and the believer who holds the faith in the face of the threat is the believer whose success the Lord grants, and the warning of the youths is the model, the declaration that the faith is the thing that the believer must not surrender. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the value of the faith: the youths warned of the loss, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose success the Lord secures, and the warning is the teaching that the apostasy is the failure. The verse is the record of the warning, and the teaching of the steadfastness that the faith demands.",
  "21": "“That is how We caused them to be discovered so that their people might know that Allah’s promise ˹of resurrection˺ is true and that there is no doubt about the Hour. When the people disputed with each other about the case of the youth ˹after their death˺, some proposed, “Build a structure around them. Their Lord knows best about them.” Those who prevailed in the matter said, “We will surely build a place of worship over them.”” records the discovery, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the discovery: “That is how We caused them to be discovered so that their people might know that Allah’s promise of resurrection is true and that there is no doubt about the Hour,” or “wa kathalika a‘tharna ‘alayhim li-ya‘lamoo anna wa‘da Allahi haqqun wa anna as-sa‘ata la rayba feeha,” and the commentators note the discovery, for the Lord caused the discovery of the youths, and the purpose is the proof, the declaration that the promise of the resurrection is the truth, and the verse is the record of the discovery, the proof that the story displays. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the discovery is the proof, and the proof is the resurrection, and the verse is the record of the discovery, the sign that the Hour is the certain. The second part is the dispute: “When the people disputed with each other about the case of the youth after their death, some proposed, ‘Build a structure around them…’ Those who prevailed in the matter said, ‘We will surely build a place of worship over them,’” or “ith yatanaza‘oona baynahum amrahum fa-qaloo ubnoo ‘alayhim bunyanan… qala allatheena ghalaboo ‘ala amrihim la-nattakhithanna ‘alayhim masjidan,” and the commentators note the dispute, for the people disputed the case, and the verse is the record of the dispute, the teaching that the difference of the people is the thing that the story records.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the proof, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the story of the cave is the proof of the resurrection, and the one who reflects upon the sleep and the raising is the one whose certainty the proof strengthens, and the believer who trusts the promise of the Lord is the believer whose faith the sign confirms, and the discovery of the youths is the teaching, the declaration that the Hour is the certain, and the believer who reads the discovery is the believer whose hope the resurrection sustains. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the Hour: the youths were discovered, and the believer who trusts the promise is the believer whose certainty the sign confirms, and the proof is the teaching that the resurrection is the truth. The verse is the record of the discovery, and the teaching of the certainty that the proof produces.",
  "22": "“Some will say, “They were three, their dog was the fourth,” while others will say, “They were five, their dog was the sixth,” ˹only˺ guessing blindly. And others will say, “They were seven and their dog was the eighth.” Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “My Lord knows best their ˹exact˺ number. Only a few people know as well.” So do not argue about them except with sure knowledge, nor consult any of those ˹who debate˺ about them.”” records the dispute of the number, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the dispute: “Some will say, ‘They were three…’ while others will say, ‘They were five…’ And others will say, ‘They were seven…’” or “sa-yaqooloona thalathatun rabi‘uhum kalbuhum… wa yaqooloona khamsatun sadisuhum kalbuhum… wa yaqooloona sab‘atun wa thaminuhum kalbuhum,” and the commentators note the dispute, for the people disputed the number of the youths, and the dispute is the guess, and the verse is the record of the dispute, the speculation that the following command corrects. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the dispute is the guess, and the guess is the ignorance, and the verse is the record of the dispute, the speculation that the Prophet was commanded to avoid. The second part is the command: “Say, ‘My Lord knows best their exact number…’ So do not argue about them except with sure knowledge, nor consult any of those who debate about them,” or “qul rabbee a‘lamu bi-‘iddatihim… fa-la tumari feehim illa mira’an zahiran wa la tastafti feehim minhum ahadan,” and the commentators note the command, for the Prophet was commanded to attribute the knowledge to the Lord, and the command is the teaching, the declaration that the exact number is the knowledge of the Lord, and the believer is not to argue the speculation.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the knowledge, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own speech. The believer is taught that the exact knowledge of the hidden is the Lord's, and the one who speculates without the knowledge is the one whose speculation the verse corrects, and the believer who attributes the knowledge to the Lord is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the command of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the believer does not argue the unknown, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose speech the command disciplines. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the discipline of the knowledge: the number is the Lord's, and the believer who attributes the knowledge to the Lord is the believer whose humility the Lord accepts, and the argument without the knowledge is the thing that the believer avoids. The verse is the record of the dispute and the command, and the teaching of the knowledge that the believer must attribute to the Lord.",
  "23": "“And never say of anything, “I will definitely do this tomorrow,”” records the first part of the command, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the command: “And never say of anything, ‘I will definitely do this tomorrow,’” or “wa la taqoolanna li-shay’in innee fa‘ilun thalika ghadan,” and the commentators note the command, for the believer is forbidden to assert the doing of the morrow without the exception, and the command is the teaching, the declaration that the future is the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the command, the first half that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the verse teaches the believer the reliance, the acknowledgment that the morrow is not the servant's. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the assertion of the morrow is the claim of the knowledge, and the claim is the error, and the verse is the record of the command, the teaching that the believer attaches the future to the will of the Lord.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reliance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own speech. The believer is taught that the future is the Lord's, and the one who asserts the morrow is the one whose claim the verse corrects, and the believer who attaches the plans to the will of the Lord is the believer whose reliance the Lord values, and the command of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the believer does not claim the morrow, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose speech the command disciplines. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reliance of the speech: the morrow is the Lord's, and the believer who attaches the future to the will is the believer whose reliance the Lord accepts, and the command is the discipline that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the reliance that the speech requires.",
  "24": "“without adding, “if Allah so wills!” But if you forget, then remember your Lord, and say, “I trust my Lord will guide me to what is more right than this.”” completes the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the exception: “without adding, ‘if Allah so wills!’” or “illa an yasha’a Allahu,” and the commentators note the exception, for the believer is to add the exception of the will of the Lord, and the exception is the “insha’ Allah,” the submission, and the verse is the record of the exception, the completion of the command, and the commentators note the significance, for the exception is the key, the declaration that the future is the Lord's, and the believer who adds the exception is the believer whose reliance the Lord values. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the exception is the submission, and the submission is the reliance, and the verse is the record of the exception, the completion of the command. The second part is the remedy: “But if you forget, then remember your Lord, and say, ‘I trust my Lord will guide me to what is more right than this,’” or “wathkur rabbaka itha naseeta wa qul ‘asa an yahdiyani rabbee li-aqraba min hatha rashadan,” and the commentators note the remedy, for the one who forgets the exception is to remember the Lord and to hope the guidance, and the verse is the record of the remedy, the teaching that the forgetfulness is repaired by the remembrance, and the commentators note the tenderness, for the Lord taught the believer the remedy of the slip.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the exception, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own speech. The believer is taught that the exception of the will is the reliance, and the one who says “if Allah wills” is the one whose speech the Lord blesses, and the believer who forgets is the believer whom the remedy repairs, and the hope of the guidance is the trust, the declaration that the Lord guides to the better, and the believer who reads the verse is the believer whose speech the command disciplines and whose slip the remedy heals. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reliance of the speech: the exception is the submission, and the believer who attaches the plans to the will is the believer whose reliance the Lord accepts, and the remedy is the mercy that the believer receives. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the exception that the reliance requires.",
  "25": "“They had remained in their cave for three hundred years, adding nine.”” records the length of the stay, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the length: “They had remained in their cave for three hundred years, adding nine,” or “wa labithoo fee kahfihim thalatha mi’atin sineena wazdadoo tis‘an,” and the commentators note the length, for the youths remained three hundred solar years, and the addition of the nine is the lunar reckoning, the three hundred and nine lunar years, and the verse is the record of the length, the teaching that the stay was the centuries, and the commentators note the significance, for the length of the stay is the proof, the declaration that the sleep of the centuries is the sign of the resurrection, and the verse is the record of the length, the proof that the following verse attributes to the knowledge of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the three hundred is the solar, and the addition of the nine is the lunar, and the verse is the record of the length, the proof of the power.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the length, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reflection. The believer is taught that the sleep of the centuries is the sign, and the one who reflects upon the length is the one whose certainty the sign strengthens, and the believer who trusts the power of the Lord is the believer whose faith the proof confirms, and the length of the stay is the teaching, the declaration that the time is the Lord's, and the believer who reads the length is the believer whose hope the resurrection sustains. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the centuries passed in the sleep, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose certainty the sign confirms, and the length is the proof that the time is the Lord's. The verse is the record of the length, and the teaching of the power that the time displays.",
  "26": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Allah knows best how long they stayed. With Him ˹alone˺ is ˹the knowledge of˺ the unseen of the heavens and the earth. How perfectly He hears and sees! They have no guardian besides Him, and He shares His command with none.”” completes the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the attribution: “Say, ‘Allah knows best how long they stayed. With Him alone is the knowledge of the unseen of the heavens and the earth. How perfectly He hears and sees!’” or “quli Allahu a‘lamu bima labithoo lahu ghaybu as-samawati wal-ardi absir bihi wa asmi‘,” and the commentators note the attribution, for the Prophet was commanded to attribute the knowledge of the stay to the Lord, and the attribution is the teaching, the declaration that the unseen is the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the attribution, the completion of the account. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the unseen is the Lord's, and the hearing and the seeing are the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the attribution, the teaching that the knowledge is the Lord's. The second part is the declaration: “They have no guardian besides Him, and He shares His command with none,” or “ma lahum min doonihi min waliyyin wa la yushriku fee hukmihi ahadan,” and the commentators note the declaration, for the Lord is the sole Guardian, and the command is the Lord's alone, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the teaching that the believer trusts the Lord alone, and the commentators note the significance, for the declaration is the key, the assurance that the Lord is the only Guardian and the only Judge.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the attribution, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the unseen is the Lord's, and the one who attributes the knowledge to the Lord is the one whose humility the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord as the sole Guardian is the believer whose trust the Lord honours, and the declaration of the verse is the teaching, the assurance that the Lord is the only protector, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose trust the declaration strengthens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the exclusivity of the trust: the unseen is the Lord's, and the believer who trusts the Lord alone is the believer whose trust the Lord accepts, and the sole command is the teaching that the believer relies upon the Lord alone. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the trust that the Lord alone deserves.",
  "27": "\"Recite what has been revealed to you from the Book of your Lord. None can change His Words, nor can you find any refuge besides Him\" instructs the Prophet and, through him, every believer to hold fast to the Quran. After the long story of the cave and the discussion of what is known and unknown, the verse turns to what is certain: the revealed Book.\n\nThe command to \"recite\" means more than reading aloud. It means reciting with understanding, acting on what is recited, and conveying it to others. The Quran is meant to be recited regularly, studied deeply, and lived by. It is not a book to be placed on a shelf and forgotten.\n\nThe phrase \"what has been revealed to you from the Book of your Lord\" emphasizes that the Quran is from God, not from the Prophet's own thinking. The Prophet did not compose it. He received it. Every word is from the Lord, which is why it deserves to be recited and followed.\n\nThe statement that \"none can change His Words\" is a powerful assurance. The words of God are protected. They cannot be altered, corrupted, or replaced. The previous chapter also stated that God Himself guards the Quran. The book in the hands of Muslims today is the same book that was revealed to the Prophet, word for word.\n\nThis protection is unique. Previous scriptures were not preserved in their original form because their protection was not guaranteed in the same way. The Quran is the final revelation, and God has taken it upon Himself to guard it.\n\nThe final clause, \"nor can you find any refuge besides Him,\" means that there is no place to escape to and no protector other than God. The One whose words cannot be changed is the only One who can shelter you. This is both a warning and a comfort. It warns against relying on anything besides God. It comforts the believer that the God they rely on is absolutely powerful and faithful.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to anchor your life in the Quran. In a world of changing opinions, shifting values, and uncertain claims, the words of God are unchanging. Recite them, study them, and seek refuge in the One who revealed them.",
  "28": "\"And patiently stick with those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, seeking His pleasure. Do not let your eyes look beyond them, desiring the luxuries of this worldly life. And do not obey those whose hearts We have made heedless of Our remembrance, who follow only their desires and whose state is total loss\" gives the Prophet and the believers guidance about whom to keep company with.\n\nThe phrase \"patiently stick with\" means to remain with someone steadfastly, despite difficulty. The people described are those who call upon their Lord morning and evening. These were the poor believers, the companions who had no wealth or status but who were devoted to worship. The Prophet is instructed to stay with them and not to turn away from them.\n\nThe instruction to seek God's pleasure is important. These people were not worshipping for show or for worldly gain. They sought God alone, in the morning and in the evening, at the beginning and end of their days. Their worship was constant and sincere.\n\nThe Prophet is told not to let his eyes look beyond them, desiring the luxuries of this worldly life. This is a rebuke to any tendency to prefer the company of the wealthy and powerful over that of the sincere poor. The leaders of Mecca had asked the Prophet to send away the poor believers so that they, the elite, could sit with him. God refused.\n\nThe worth of a person is not measured by their wealth or appearance. The poor believers who sat with the Prophet were worth more in the sight of God than the arrogant chiefs who rejected the message. The Prophet was instructed to keep their company and not to trade it for the attention of the worldly.\n\nThe second half of the verse warns against obeying those whose hearts are heedless of God's remembrance. Such people follow their own desires, and their end is loss. To obey them would mean turning away from the truth to please people who have no concern for the Hereafter.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a clear instruction about friendship and company. Keep the company of those who remember God, even if they are poor or unimpressive by worldly standards. Be wary of those who are heedless, even if they are wealthy or influential. The company you keep shapes your heart, and the people you sit with determine much of your spiritual direction.",
  "29": "\"And say, O Prophet, 'This is the truth from your Lord. Whoever wills let them believe, and whoever wills let them disbelieve.' Surely We have prepared for the wrongdoers a Fire whose walls will completely surround them. When they cry for aid, they will be aided with water like molten metal, which will burn their faces. What a horrible drink! And what a terrible place to rest!\" presents the choice between belief and disbelief with full clarity about the consequences.\n\nThe opening statement, \"This is the truth from your Lord,\" establishes the source and nature of the message. It is not the Prophet's opinion, a cultural tradition, or a human philosophy. It is the truth from the Lord of all. The Prophet's job is to deliver it, not to force it.\n\nThe choice that follows is one of the most powerful statements of human freedom in the Quran: \"Whoever wills let them believe, and whoever wills let them disbelieve.\" This does not mean that God is indifferent to what people choose. It means that no one is forced. The truth has been made clear. The decision belongs to each person. With that decision comes responsibility.\n\nThe verse then describes the consequence for the wrongdoers: a Fire whose walls surround them. The image is of complete enclosure. There is no escape. The walls of Hell are not just physical barriers. They represent the inescapable result of a life of wrongdoing.\n\nWhen the people of Hell cry for help, they are given water \"like molten metal.\" This is not relief. It is a punishment that burns their faces. The description is intentionally severe. The Quran uses vivid imagery to make the reality of Hell feel present to the reader. People are naturally moved by images more than abstract statements.\n\nThe two exclamations at the end, \"What a horrible drink!\" and \"What a terrible place to rest!\" express disgust at the fate of the wrongdoers. The drink does not quench thirst. The resting place does not provide rest. Everything in Hell is the opposite of what a person would hope for.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a clear presentation of the choice. You have been told the truth. You are free to accept or reject it. But you are not free to choose the consequences. The Fire is real, and the only protection from it is belief in the truth that has been presented to you.",
  "30": "\"As for those who believe and do good, We certainly never deny the reward of those who are best in deeds\" shifts from the description of Hell to the reward of the righteous. The previous verse ended with the horror of the Fire. This verse opens with the promise of Paradise.\n\nThe phrase \"believe and do good\" is the Quran's constant formula for salvation. Belief alone, if it produces no action, is not the faith the Quran describes. Good deeds without belief are not accepted as the basis of salvation. The two must come together: conviction in the heart and action in the limbs.\n\nThe statement that God \"never denies the reward\" is an absolute assurance. The good deeds of the righteous will not be lost, diminished, or forgotten. Every prayer, every act of charity, every kind word, and every moment of patience is recorded and will be fully rewarded.\n\nThe phrase \"those who are best in deeds\" emphasizes quality, not just quantity. As mentioned earlier in the chapter, the test of life is to see who is best in deeds, not who has the most. The best deed is the one done with sincerity, in accordance with the Prophet's way, and with awareness of God.\n\nThis verse is a comfort to believers who may feel that their efforts are small or unnoticed. In this world, good deeds often go unrewarded and unacknowledged. People may not see them, may not appreciate them, or may even criticize them. But God sees them, and He does not deny their reward.\n\nIt is also a motivation. The fact that the reward is certain should encourage the believer to keep doing good, even when it is difficult. The hardship of obedience is temporary, but the reward is eternal. A single good deed done sincerely may be the cause of eternal happiness.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to examine your deeds. Are they done with belief in God? Are they done in the best way you can manage? If so, take comfort. God does not waste the reward of those who do good. He may delay it, but He will not deny it.",
  "31": "\"It is they who will have the Gardens of Eternity, with rivers flowing under their feet. There they will be adorned with bracelets of gold, and wear green garments of fine silk and rich brocade, reclining there on canopied couches. What a marvellous reward! And what a fabulous place to rest!\" describes the reward of Paradise in vivid, sensory detail. The previous verse said that God does not deny the reward. This verse shows what that reward is.\n\nThe \"Gardens of Eternity\" are the home of the righteous. The word \"eternity\" establishes that this reward is permanent, unlike the gardens of this world, which fade and die. The gardens of Paradise never end.\n\nThe image of rivers flowing under their feet is one of the most common descriptions of Paradise in the Quran. Water is the source of life, and in Paradise, it flows continuously. The rivers do not stagnate or dry up. They are part of the beauty and sustenance of the Garden.\n\nThe believers are adorned with bracelets of gold. Gold is the most precious metal in this world, and in Paradise it is worn as jewelry, a sign of honor and beauty. The fact that men are described wearing bracelets of gold, which is forbidden for men in this life, shows that the rules of this world do not apply to the next in every detail. What is a test here is an adornment there.\n\nThe garments are green, made of fine silk and rich brocade. Green is associated with life, growth, and freshness. Silk and brocade represent luxury and comfort. The clothing of Paradise is not like the rough clothing of this world. It is fine, beautiful, and eternally comfortable.\n\nThe believers recline on canopied couches. This is an image of complete rest and ease. There is no fatigue, no standing in exhaustion, no uncomfortable position. They are at peace, sheltered by canopies and surrounded by beauty.\n\nThe two exclamations at the end mirror the exclamations about Hell in the previous verse. There, the drink was horrible and the resting place was terrible. Here, the reward is marvellous and the resting place is fabulous. The contrast is deliberate and complete.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a motivation. The pleasures described are beyond what any human being has experienced. They are real, and they are promised to those who believe and do good. Keep that promise in mind when obedience feels difficult.",
  "32": "“Give them ˹O Prophet˺ an example of two men. To ˹the disbelieving˺ one We gave two gardens of grapevines, which We surrounded with palm trees and placed ˹various˺ crops in between.”” opens the parable of the two men, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse commands the parable: “Give them O Prophet an example of two men,” or “wadrib lahum mathalan rajulayni,” and the commentators note the command, for the Prophet was commanded to strike the parable of the two men, and the parable is the teaching, the comparison of the grateful and the ungrateful, and the verse then describes the first: “To the disbelieving one We gave two gardens of grapevines, which We surrounded with palm trees and placed various crops in between,” or “ja‘alna li-ahadihima jannatayni min a‘nabin wa hafafnahuma bi-nakhlin wa ja‘alna baynahuma zar‘an,” and the commentators note the description, for the Lord gave the disbeliever the two gardens, and the description is the bounty, the wealth that the man received, and the verse is the record of the parable, the opening of the story that the following verses unfold. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the two gardens are the bounty, and the bounty is the test, and the verse is the record of the parable, the introduction of the contrast.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the parable, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own gratitude. The believer is taught that the bounty is the test, and the one who receives the bounty is the one whose gratitude the bounty tests, and the believer who is given the wealth is the believer whose gratitude the Lord weighs, and the parable of the two men is the teaching, the comparison of the grateful and the ungrateful, and the believer who reads the parable is the believer whose gratitude the comparison sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the test of the bounty: the two gardens were the test, and the believer who is given the wealth is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the parable is the teaching that the wealth is the trial. The verse is the record of the parable, and the teaching of the gratitude that the bounty tests.",
  "33": "“Each garden yielded ˹all˺ its produce, never falling short. And We caused a river to flow between them.”” continues the description, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse continues the description: “Each garden yielded all its produce, never falling short. And We caused a river to flow between them,” or “kiltal-jannatayni atat ukulaha wa lam tazlim minhu shay’an wa fajjarna khilalahuma naharan,” and the commentators note the two clauses, the fullness of the yield and the flow of the river, and the verse is the record of the description, the completeness of the bounty, and the commentators note the significance, for the gardens that never fell short and the river that flowed between are the symbol of the abundance, and the abundance is the test, and the verse is the record of the description, the bounty that the following verses contrast with the arrogance. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the yield and the river are the abundance, and the abundance is the test, and the verse is the record of the description, the bounty that the man received.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the bounty, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own gratitude. The believer is taught that the abundance is the test, and the one who receives the abundance is the one whose gratitude the abundance tests, and the believer who is given the provision is the believer whose gratitude the Lord weighs, and the description of the gardens is the teaching, the declaration that the bounty is the complete, and the believer who reads the description is the believer whose gratitude the bounty sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completeness of the bounty: the yield and the river, and the believer who is given the provision is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the bounty is the trial that the following verses unfold. The verse is the record of the description, and the teaching of the gratitude that the abundance tests.",
  "34": "“And he had other resources ˹as well˺. So he boasted to a ˹poor˺ companion of his, while conversing with him, “I am greater than you in wealth and superior in manpower.”” records the boast, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the resources: “And he had other resources as well,” or “wa kana lahu thamarun,” and the commentators note the resources, for the man had the other fruits and the wealth, and the verse is the record of the resources, the completeness of the bounty. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the resources are the wealth, and the wealth is the trial, and the verse is the record of the resources, the bounty that the boast abused. The second part is the boast: “So he boasted to a poor companion of his, while conversing with him, ‘I am greater than you in wealth and superior in manpower,’” or “fa-qala li-sahibihi wa huwa yuhawiruhu ana aktharu minka malan wa a‘azzu nafaran,” and the commentators note the boast, for the man boasted of the wealth and the manpower, and the boast is the arrogance, the first sin, and the verse is the record of the boast, the arrogance that the following verses condemn, and the commentators note the significance, for the boast is the corruption of the bounty, the turning of the gift into the pride, and the verse is the record of the boast, the sin that the parable teaches.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the boast, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own humility. The believer is taught that the boast is the corruption of the bounty, and the one who boasts of the wealth is the one whose pride the Lord condemns, and the believer who is given the wealth is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the boast of the man is the warning, the declaration that the wealth is the trial, and the believer who reads the boast is the believer whose humility the warning sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the wealth: the man boasted, and the believer who is given the wealth is the believer whose humility the Lord tests, and the boast is the sin that the parable warns against. The verse is the record of the boast, and the teaching of the humility that the wealth requires.",
  "35": "“And he entered his property, while wronging his soul, saying, “I do not think this will ever perish,”” records the first claim, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the entry: “And he entered his property, while wronging his soul,” or “wa dakhala jannatahu wa huwa zalimun li-nafsihi,” and the commentators note the entry, for the man entered the garden, and the verse declares the self-wronging, the “zulm,” and the verse is the record of the entry, the description of the state. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the entry is the self-wronging, and the wrong is the arrogance, and the verse is the record of the entry, the state that the following claims reveal. The second part is the claim: “I do not think this will ever perish,” or “qala ma azunnu an tabeeda hathihi abadan,” and the commentators note the claim, for the man declared that the garden would never perish, and the claim is the first error, the denial of the perishability of the world, and the verse is the record of the claim, the arrogance that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the claim, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the world is the perishable, and the one who denies the perishability is the one whose denial the parable condemns, and the believer who knows the end of the world is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the claim of the man is the warning, the declaration that the denial of the end is the error, and the believer who reads the claim is the believer whose certainty the warning sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the perishability of the world: the man denied the end, and the believer who knows the end is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the claim is the error that the parable warns against. The verse is the record of the claim, and the teaching of the certainty that the perishability requires.",
  "36": "“nor do I think the Hour will ˹ever˺ come. And if in fact I am returned to my Lord, I will definitely get a far better outcome than ˹all˺ this.”” completes the claims, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the denial: “nor do I think the Hour will ever come,” or “wa ma azunnu as-sa‘ata qa’imatan,” and the commentators note the denial, for the man denied the Hour, and the denial is the second error, the disbelief in the resurrection, and the verse is the record of the denial, the error that the following clause compounds. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the denial of the Hour is the disbelief, and the disbelief is the error, and the verse is the record of the denial, the error that the claim of the return contradicts. The second part is the presumption: “And if in fact I am returned to my Lord, I will definitely get a far better outcome than all this,” or “wa la-in rudidtu ila rabbee la-ajidanna khayran minha munqalaban,” and the commentators note the presumption, for the man claimed that the return would bring the better, and the presumption is the third error, the claim of the merit, and the verse is the record of the presumption, the arrogance that the following verse answers with the companion's reply.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the presumption, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own humility. The believer is taught that the presumption of the merit is the error, and the one who denies the Hour and claims the better is the one whose presumption the parable condemns, and the believer who hopes the mercy is the believer whose hope the Lord values, and the presumption of the man is the warning, the declaration that the denial and the claim are the two errors, and the believer who reads the presumption is the believer whose humility the warning sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the error of the presumption: the man denied and claimed, and the believer who hopes in the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord accepts, and the presumption is the arrogance that the parable warns against. The verse is the record of the claims, and the teaching of the humility that the presumption opposes.",
  "37": "“His ˹believing˺ companion replied, while conversing with him, “Do you disbelieve in the One Who created you from dust, then ˹developed you˺ from a sperm-drop, then formed you into a man?”” records the reply of the companion, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: “His believing companion replied, while conversing with him, ‘Do you disbelieve in the One Who created you from dust, then developed you from a sperm-drop, then formed you into a man?’” or “qala lahu sahibuhu wa huwa yuhawiruhu a-kafarta billathee khalaqaka min turabin thumma min nutfatin thumma sawwaka rajulan,” and the commentators note the reply, for the companion answered the claims with the reminder of the creation, and the reminder is the argument, the declaration that the One who created the man from the dust and the drop is the One whom the man denied, and the verse is the record of the reply, the argument that the following verses develop. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reminder of the creation is the argument, and the argument is the truth, and the verse is the record of the reply, the correction of the denial.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reminder, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own gratitude. The believer is taught that the remembrance of the creation is the argument of the truth, and the one who denies the Creator is the one whose denial the reminder refutes, and the believer who remembers the origin is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the reply of the companion is the model, the reminder of the creation that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose gratitude the reminder sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the creation: the companion reminded the origin, and the believer who remembers the dust is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the reminder is the correction of the denial. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the gratitude that the remembrance produces.",
  "38": "“But as for me: He is Allah, my Lord, and I will never associate anyone with my Lord ˹in worship˺.”” continues the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse continues the reply: “But as for me: He is Allah, my Lord, and I will never associate anyone with my Lord in worship,” or “lakinna huwa Allahu rabbee wa la ushriku bi-rabbee ahadan,” and the commentators note the declaration, for the companion declared the oneness, and the declaration is the faith, the statement of the monotheism, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the faith that the following verse applies, and the commentators note the significance, for the declaration is the contrast, the faith of the companion against the denial of the man, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the faith that the parable upholds. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the declaration is the monotheism, and the monotheism is the truth, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the faith that the companion held.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the declaration, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own faith. The believer is taught that the declaration of the oneness is the faith, and the one who declares the Lord is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the monotheism is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the declaration of the companion is the model, the statement of the oneness that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the declaration is the believer whose faith the model strengthens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the faith of the monotheism: the companion declared the Lord, and the believer who holds the oneness is the believer whose faith the Lord accepts, and the declaration is the contrast with the denial of the man. The verse is the record of the declaration, and the teaching of the faith that the companion held.",
  "39": "“If only you had said, upon entering your property, ‘This is what Allah has willed! There is no power except with Allah!’ Even though you see me inferior to you in wealth and offspring,” continues the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the correction: “If only you had said, upon entering your property, ‘This is what Allah has willed! There is no power except with Allah!’” or “wa law la ith dakhalta jannataka qulta ma sha’a Allahu la quwwata illa billah,” and the commentators note the correction, for the companion taught the man the word of the gratitude, and the correction is the teaching, the declaration that the bounty is the will of the Lord, and the verse is the record of the correction, the teaching that the following clause applies. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the word of the will is the gratitude, and the gratitude is the remedy, and the verse is the record of the correction, the teaching of the word. The second part is the admission: “Even though you see me inferior to you in wealth and offspring,” or “in tarani ana aqalla minka malan wa waladan,” and the commentators note the admission, for the companion acknowledged the inferiority of the wealth, and the admission is the humility, the declaration that the worth is not the wealth, and the verse is the record of the admission, the humility that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the word, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own speech. The believer is taught that the word of the will is the gratitude, and the one who attributes the bounty to the Lord is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who says “what Allah has willed” is the believer whose speech the Lord blesses, and the correction of the companion is the teaching, the declaration that the bounty is the Lord's, and the believer who reads the correction is the believer whose speech the teaching disciplines. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the word of the gratitude: the companion taught the word, and the believer who attributes the bounty to the Lord is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the word is the remedy of the boast. The verse is the record of the correction, and the teaching of the word that the gratitude requires.",
  "40": "“perhaps my Lord will grant me ˹something˺ better than your garden, and send down upon your garden a thunderbolt from the sky, turning it into a barren waste.”” records the hope and the warning, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the hope: “perhaps my Lord will grant me something better than your garden,” or “fa-‘asa rabbee an yu’tiyani khayran min jannatika,” and the commentators note the hope, for the companion hoped the better from the Lord, and the hope is the trust, the reliance upon the Lord, and the verse is the record of the hope, the trust that the following clause completes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the hope is the trust, and the trust is the faith, and the verse is the record of the hope, the reliance of the companion. The second part is the warning: “and send down upon your garden a thunderbolt from the sky, turning it into a barren waste,” or “wa yursila ‘alayha husbanan mina as-sama’i fa-tusbiha sa‘eedan zalaqan,” and the commentators note the warning, for the companion warned that the Lord could send the thunderbolt, and the warning is the teaching, the declaration that the bounty is the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the warning, the teaching that the following verses fulfill.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the warning, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own fear. The believer is taught that the bounty is the Lord's, and the one who boasts of the bounty is the one whose boast the Lord answers, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord rewards, and the warning of the companion is the teaching, the declaration that the thunderbolt is the power of the Lord, and the believer who reads the warning is the believer whose fear the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the companion warned of the thunderbolt, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the warning is the teaching that the bounty is the Lord's. The verse is the record of the hope and the warning, and the teaching of the power that the bounty serves.",
  "41": "“Or its water may sink ˹into the earth˺, and then you will never be able to seek it out.”” completes the warning, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the warning: “Or its water may sink into the earth, and then you will never be able to seek it out,” or “aw yusbiha ma’uha ghawran fa-lan tastatee‘a lahu talaban,” and the commentators note the warning, for the companion warned that the water could sink, and the warning is the teaching, the declaration that the means of the bounty are the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the warning, the teaching that the following verse fulfills, and the commentators note the significance, for the two warnings — the thunderbolt and the sinking — are the two ways that the Lord removes the bounty, and the verse is the record of the warning, the teaching that the bounty is the trust. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the sinking of the water is the removal, and the removal is the power, and the verse is the record of the warning, the teaching of the power of the Lord.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the warning, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the means of the bounty are the Lord's, and the one who trusts the means is the one whose trust the Lord tests, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord rewards, and the warning of the companion is the teaching, the declaration that the bounty is the trust, and the believer who reads the warning is the believer whose trust the teaching corrects. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the fragility of the means: the water could sink, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the warning is the teaching that the bounty is the Lord's. The verse is the record of the warning, and the teaching of the trust that the bounty demands.",
  "42": "“And so all his produce was ˹totally˺ ruined, so he started to wring his hands for all he had spent on it, while it had collapsed on its trellises. He cried, “Alas! I wish I had never associated anyone with my Lord ˹in worship˺!”” records the ruin and the regret, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the ruin: “And so all his produce was totally ruined, so he started to wring his hands for all he had spent on it, while it had collapsed on its trellises,” or “wa uheeta bi-thamarihi fa-asbaha yuqallibu kaffayhi ‘ala ma anfaqa feeha wa hiya khawiyatun ‘ala ‘urooshiha,” and the commentators note the ruin, for the produce was ruined and the garden collapsed, and the wringing of the hands is the regret, and the verse is the record of the ruin, the fulfillment of the warning. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the ruin is the fulfillment, and the fulfillment is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the ruin, the end of the boast. The second part is the regret: “He cried, ‘Alas! I wish I had never associated anyone with my Lord in worship!’” or “yaqoolu ya laytanee lam ushrik bi-rabbee ahadan,” and the commentators note the regret, for the man cried the regret of the association, and the regret is the late repentance, the confession that came at the ruin, and the verse is the record of the regret, the teaching that the return at the ruin is the late.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the ruin, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own gratitude. The believer is taught that the ruin of the bounty is the warning, and the one who boasts of the bounty is the one whose boast the ruin answers, and the believer who is grateful is the believer whose bounty the Lord preserves, and the regret of the man is the teaching, the declaration that the association is the loss, and the believer who reads the ruin is the believer whose gratitude the warning sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the boast: the garden collapsed and the man regretted, and the believer who is grateful is the believer whose bounty the Lord preserves, and the regret is the late repentance that the believer must avoid by the early gratitude. The verse is the record of the ruin and the regret, and the teaching of the gratitude that the bounty requires.",
  "43": "“And he had no manpower to help him against Allah, nor could he ˹even˺ help himself.”” records the helplessness, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the helplessness: “And he had no manpower to help him against Allah, nor could he even help himself,” or “wa lam takun lahu fi’atun yansuroonahu min dooni Allahi wa ma kana muntasiran,” and the commentators note the two clauses, the absence of the manpower and the absence of the self-help, and the verse is the record of the helplessness, the teaching that the boast of the manpower was the illusion, and the commentators note the significance, for the man who had boasted of the “a‘azzu nafaran” — the superior manpower — now had no one, and the verse is the record of the helplessness, the exposure of the boast. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the manpower is the thing that the boast claimed, and the absence is the exposure, and the verse is the record of the helplessness, the teaching that the strength of the world is the illusion.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the helplessness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The believer is taught that the strength of the world is the illusion, and the one who trusts the manpower is the one whose trust the ruin exposes, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose reliance the Lord rewards, and the helplessness of the man is the warning, the declaration that the refuge of the world is the refuge that fails, and the believer who reads the helplessness is the believer whose reliance the warning corrects. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the vanity of the strength: the man had no helper, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose reliance the Lord confirms, and the helplessness is the teaching that the strength of the world is the illusion. The verse is the record of the helplessness, and the teaching of the reliance that the Lord alone deserves.",
  "44": "“At this time, support comes ˹only˺ from Allah—the True ˹Lord˺. He is best in reward and best in outcome.”” completes the parable, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the declaration: “At this time, support comes only from Allah—the True Lord,” or “hunalika al-walayatu lillahi al-haqqi,” and the commentators note the declaration, for the support is the Lord's, and the declaration is the teaching, the statement that the true protection is the Lord, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the teaching that the following clause completes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the support is the Lord's, and the Lord is the Truth, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the teaching of the true reliance. The second part is the praise: “He is best in reward and best in outcome,” or “huwa khayrun thawaban wa khayrun ‘uqban,” and the commentators note the praise, for the Lord is the best in the reward and the outcome, and the verse is the record of the praise, the teaching that the Lord's reward and the Lord's end are the better, and the commentators note the significance, for the parable closes with the declaration that the trust of the believer is the Lord, and the believer's end is the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching of the parable.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the support, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the support is the Lord's, and the one who trusts the Lord is the one whose support the Lord grants, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose reward and outcome the Lord makes the best, and the declaration of the verse is the teaching, the assurance that the Lord is the true protector, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose trust the declaration strengthens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the true support: the Lord is the support, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose end the Lord makes the best, and the parable is the teaching that the bounty is the test and the Lord is the support. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the trust that the parable inculcates.",
  "45": "\"And give them a parable of this worldly life. It is like the plants of the earth, thriving when sustained by the rain We send down from the sky. Then they soon turn into chaff scattered by the wind. And Allah is fully capable of doing all things\" broadens the lesson of the garden into a general parable of all worldly life. The previous verses concluded the story of the two men. This verse applies its lesson to everything in the world.\n\nThe comparison to plants is familiar and powerful. Rain falls from the sky, the earth turns green, plants grow tall and beautiful, and the fields look abundant. People see the growth and assume it will last. But then the plants dry up, turn into straw, and the wind scatters them. The lush landscape becomes scattered debris.\n\nThis is exactly what happened to the wealthy man's garden, and it is what happens to every worldly thing. Youth turns to old age. Wealth turns to loss. Power turns to defeat. Life turns to death. The beauty that seemed so solid is revealed to be as fragile as grass.\n\nThe word \"chaff\" refers to the dry, broken pieces of plant material that have no value and are easily blown away. The Quran uses this image to describe the end of worldly things. What was once thriving becomes worthless and scattered. The contrast between the green plant and the dry chaff is the contrast between this world and the Hereafter.\n\nThe wind that scatters the chaff is a reminder of God's power. He sends the rain that gives life, and He sends the wind that takes it away. He is fully capable of both causing growth and causing decay. Nothing in the natural world, and nothing in human life, exists independently of His will.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to see the world as it really is. The things that consume people's attention, money, appearance, status, and pleasure, are like plants after rain: beautiful for a moment, then scattered. This does not mean that worldly life is evil or that believers should refuse to enjoy it. It means that they should see it for what it is and use it as a field for the next life.\n\nA person who understands this parable does not stop planting. They plant, but they do not fall in love with the crop. They give thanks while the plants are green and remain patient when the wind comes. Their hearts are attached to the One who sends the rain, not to the plants that grow from it.",
  "46": "\"Wealth and children are the adornment of this worldly life, but the everlasting good deeds are far better with your Lord in reward and in hope\" draws a distinction between the temporary adornments of this world and the deeds that last forever. The previous verse compared worldly life to plants that become chaff. This verse names two of the most beloved adornments and then points to what is better.\n\nWealth and children are described as the \"adornment\" of worldly life. The word for adornment, \"zinah,\" means something that beautifies and makes life attractive. Money makes life comfortable and allows for generosity. Children bring joy, continuity, and love. These things are not evil in themselves. They are genuine blessings from God.\n\nBut they are adornments, not foundations. A decoration is meant to enhance something, not to be the thing itself. Wealth and children make worldly life beautiful, but they cannot provide its ultimate meaning or security. They can be lost, and they will be left behind at death.\n\nThe phrase \"everlasting good deeds\" refers to actions whose reward continues after a person dies: ongoing charity, beneficial knowledge, righteous children who pray for their parents, and every good deed whose effects continue. It also refers to the good works a person does in their lifetime, which remain with them in the Hereafter.\n\nThe comparison is between what adorns this life and what lasts into the next. Wealth can be taken away. Children can die or stray. Good deeds, once done sincerely for God, remain. They are \"far better with your Lord in reward and in hope.\" Their reward is greater, and the hope they give is more certain.\n\nThe Prophet taught that when a person dies, their deeds come to an end except for three: ongoing charity, knowledge that benefits people, and a righteous child who prays for them. This hadith explains what everlasting good deeds mean in practice.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to invest in what lasts. Provide for your family, love your children, and enjoy lawful wealth, but do not stop there. Spend in charity, leave behind knowledge that helps others, and raise children who will remember God and pray for you. Those are the treasures that outlive you.",
  "47": "\"Beware of the Day We will blow the mountains away, and you will see the earth laid bare. And We will gather all humankind, leaving none behind\" describes one of the terrifying events of the Day of Judgment. The previous verse spoke of what lasts in the Hereafter. This verse gives a vivid picture of the world being transformed for that Day.\n\nThe blowing on the Trumpet is a recurring image in the Quran. It refers to the angel Israfil blowing the trumpet to signal the end of the world and the resurrection. At that blow, the mountains, which seem the most permanent features of the earth, will be blown away like scattered dust or wool.\n\nThe image of mountains being removed is meant to shatter every illusion of permanence. If mountains, which have stood for thousands of years, can be blown away, then nothing in this world is secure. The earth that people build on, fight over, and take for granted will be laid completely bare.\n\n\"Laid bare\" means leveled, empty, and exposed. There will be no buildings, no trees, no valleys, no hiding places. Every feature that people used for shelter or identity will be gone. The earth will be a flat plain on which all humanity stands.\n\nThe gathering is complete: \"We will gather all humankind, leaving none behind.\" Every person who ever lived, from Adam to the last human being, will be resurrected and brought to the standing place of judgment. No one will be overlooked, no matter how remote their life or how long ago they died.\n\nThe verse uses the word \"beware\" to put the listener on alert. This Day is not a distant fable. It is a certain reality. Every person reading these words will be present on that Day. The mountains they see in the distance will be gone, and they will stand on the bare earth before God.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to prepare. The Day is coming. The mountains of your life, your home, your career, your reputation, and your strength, will all be removed. What will remain is your deeds and your relationship with God. Invest now in what will survive that Day.",
  "48": "\"They will be presented before your Lord in rows, and the deniers will be told, 'You have surely returned to Us all alone as We created you the first time, although you always claimed that We would never appoint a time for your return'\" describes the scene after humanity is gathered. The previous verse said all people will be gathered. This verse shows how they will stand and what they will be told.\n\nThe presentation in rows means that all humanity will stand in ordered lines before God. There will be no chaos, no running, no hiding. The rows reflect the perfect order of the Day. Every person stands in their place, waiting for judgment.\n\nThe phrase \"all alone\" is significant. People will be resurrected without their wealth, their children, their tribes, their armies, and their status. They will come as individuals, just as they were created. A king will stand without his crown. A billionaire will stand without his money. A celebrity will stand without their fame. The only thing each person will have is their deeds.\n\nThe comparison to the first creation is an argument for resurrection itself. God created human beings the first time from nothing. To bring them back a second time is not difficult for Him. The deniers are told that they have returned exactly as they were created: alone, bare, and entirely dependent on God.\n\nThe rebuke, \"you always claimed that We would never appoint a time for your return,\" refers to their denial of the Hereafter in this life. They lived as if there would be no accounting, no resurrection, and no judgment. Now they see with their own eyes that the time they denied has arrived.\n\nThe phrase \"appoint a time\" emphasizes that the Day was fixed by God. It was not random or optional. It was always going to happen, exactly when God decreed. The deniers' claims to the contrary did not change the reality.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder of how you will meet God: alone, with nothing but your deeds. The things you rely on in this life, your money, your connections, your identity, will not accompany you. Prepare for that meeting by building a record of good deeds that you can take with you.",
  "49": "\"And the record of deeds will be laid open, and you will see the wicked in fear of what is written in it. They will cry, 'Woe to us! What kind of record is this that does not leave any sin, small or large, unlisted?' They will find whatever they did present before them. And your Lord will never wrong anyone\" describes the opening of the book of deeds on the Day of Judgment.\n\nThe record is \"laid open,\" meaning it is visible and accessible. Every person's deeds are written in a book by the noble scribes, the angels who record everything. On the Day of Judgment, that book is brought forth and opened. Nothing is hidden.\n\nThe wicked are described as being in fear of what is written. They know what they did, but seeing it written down, complete and undeniable, fills them with terror. The record does not merely list major crimes. It leaves no sin, \"small or large,\" unlisted. Every word, every glance, every action, and every intention is there.\n\nTheir cry, \"Woe to us!\" is an expression of despair. They thought some of their deeds were too small to matter, too private to be recorded, or too long ago to be remembered. The record proves them wrong. It lists everything, from the great acts of injustice to the small wrongs they committed without thinking.\n\nThe word \"present\" means the deeds are right there before them, not as distant memories but as living realities. They cannot deny them. They cannot argue them away. The book is a witness against them, written by angels who never err.\n\nThe final statement, \"your Lord will never wrong anyone,\" is a crucial assurance. The record is accurate. The judgment is just. No one will be punished for something they did not do. No one will have their good deeds diminished. The fear of the wicked is not because God is unfair. It is because their own deeds are truly worthy of fear.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is both a warning and a comfort. It is a warning because every small and large deed is recorded. It is a comfort because God does not wrong anyone. If your deeds are good, they will be honored. If you have sinned, the door of repentance is open now, while there is still time to change what is written in the record through sincere repentance.",
  "50": "\"And remember when We said to the angels, 'Prostrate before Adam,' so they all did—but not Iblis, who was one of the jinn, but he rebelled against the command of his Lord. Would you then take him and his descendants as patrons instead of Me, although they are your enemy? What an evil alternative for the wrongdoers to choose!\" recalls the story of Satan's refusal to prostrate to Adam. The previous verses described the record of deeds. This verse goes back to the origin of evil and the source of wrongdoing.\n\nThe angels were commanded to prostrate to Adam as an act of honor, not worship. They all obeyed, except Iblis, who is Satan. The verse clarifies that he was one of the jinn, not an angel. The angels do not have free will in the same way and do not disobey God. Iblis was a jinn who had risen to a high station through worship but chose to rebel.\n\nHis rebellion was against the direct command of his Lord. He refused to prostrate to Adam out of pride, claiming that he was better because he was created from fire while Adam was created from clay. This pride was the first sin, and it has been the source of every sin since.\n\nThe question that follows is sharp: \"Would you then take him and his descendants as patrons instead of Me?\" God is pointing out the absurdity of human beings following Satan when Satan is their declared enemy. The one who refuses to honor their father Adam is hardly fit to be their guide and protector.\n\nThe word \"patrons\" means allies, protectors, and authorities. People take Satan as a patron when they follow his whispers, obey their own desires against God's commands, and give their loyalty to the path of temptation. They choose as their guide the very being who wants to lead them to Hell.\n\nThe description of this as an \"evil alternative\" expresses how terrible the choice is. Human beings were created to be served by the angels and to worship God. Instead, many choose to follow the jinn who rebelled against God and envied Adam. It is a trade of the best guide for the worst enemy.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a clear call to examine whose path you are following. Every person has a patron: either God, through His messengers and revelation, or Satan, through temptation and desire. The choice you make in this life determines your destination. Choosing God's path is the path of honor. Choosing Satan's path is the evil alternative.",
  "51": "\"I never called them to witness the creation of the heavens and the earth or even their own creation, nor would I take the misleaders as helpers\" rejects the idea that the false gods and Satan had any share in creation or in God's plan. The previous verse said people take Satan and his descendants as patrons. This verse shows how little those patrons deserve to be followed.\n\nThe phrase \"I never called them to witness\" means that God did not consult the false gods, the jinn, or anyone else when He created the heavens and the earth. Creation was His act alone. He did not need advisors, partners, or helpers.\n\nThe phrase \"or even their own creation\" adds another dimension. The false gods and the misleaders were themselves created. They did not even witness their own creation, let alone the creation of the universe. How can a created being, who did not even make itself, be taken as a lord alongside the Creator?\n\nThe statement that God would never take the misleaders as helpers makes it clear that Satan and his kind have no role in running the universe. They are not God's deputies or assistants. They are creatures who rebelled and were given respite until the Day of Judgment. They have no authority except over those who choose to follow them.\n\nThe word \"misleaders\" is important. It describes exactly what Satan and his followers do: they lead people astray. They do not guide, protect, or benefit. They mislead, deceive, and destroy. A God who is truly wise and just would never take misleaders as His helpers.\n\nThe verse also refutes any idea that Satan is an equal and opposite force to God, as some belief systems teach. There is no cosmic struggle between two equal powers. God is the sole Creator and Ruler. Satan is a creature, allowed to operate for a time within limits set by God. He has no share in divinity.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to worship the One who alone is worthy. The God who created the universe without help is the only One who deserves your devotion. The created beings who mislead deserve no worship, no following, and no loyalty. To follow them is to follow the blind who cannot even see their own creation.",
  "52": "\"And beware of the Day He will say, 'Call upon those you claimed were My associate-gods.' So they will call them, but will receive no response. And We will make them all share in the same doom\" describes the futility of calling on false gods on the Day of Judgment. The previous verse established that those false gods had no share in creation. This verse shows that they cannot even answer prayer.\n\nOn the Day of Judgment, God will command the polytheists to call upon the partners they associated with Him. They will call on the idols, the saints, the angels, the jinn, and anyone else they worshipped besides God. But none of them will respond. The idols cannot speak. The created beings who are themselves being judged cannot intercede without God's permission.\n\nThe phrase \"will receive no response\" is devastating. In this life, people may feel that their prayers to false gods are answered through coincidence or imagination. On the Day of Judgment, there will be no more illusions. The false gods will be unable to help or even answer.\n\nThe statement that God will make them \"all share in the same doom\" means that the false gods and those who worshipped them will all be in Hell together. The leaders who misled will share the punishment with the followers who were misled. No one will escape because they were only following someone else.\n\nThis does not mean that righteous human beings who were wrongly worshipped will be punished. The Prophet Jesus, for example, will dissociate himself from those who claimed he was divine. The verse refers to those who willingly led others astray and those who chose to follow them against the truth.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning about where you place your ultimate trust. The people you follow, the ideologies you adopt, the desires you obey, and the figures you admire may seem powerful in this life. But on the Day when you need them most, they will not answer. The only One who can answer is God, and He responds to those who call upon Him sincerely and alone.\n\nCall now, in this life, on the One who can actually answer. Do not wait until the Day when the false gods are exposed and the door of repentance has closed.",
  "53": "\"The wicked will see the Fire and realize that they are bound to fall into it, and will find no way to avoid it\" describes the moment the people of Hell see their destination. The previous verse said the false gods cannot help. This verse shows the wicked facing the inevitable reality of the Fire.\n\nThe word \"realize\" means they know with certainty, not just suspect. When they see the Fire, they understand that they are going to fall into it. There is no more room for denial or wishful thinking. The thing they mocked and denied is now before them.\n\nThe phrase \"bound to fall into it\" means they know it is unavoidable. There is no escape route, no excuse, and no one to intercede. The Fire is not a possibility from which they might turn away. It is the certain end of the path they chose.\n\nThey \"find no way to avoid it.\" In this life, people are skilled at avoiding consequences. They hire lawyers, flee countries, use their wealth, and call in favors. On the Day of Judgment, none of those avenues exist. Every path leads to the Fire for those who earned it.\n\nThe verse is short and stark, without detailed descriptions of the Fire itself. The horror is in the certainty: they see it and know they are falling into it. The terror of that realization is greater than any physical description.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to avoid the Fire before you see it. In this life, the Fire is hidden behind the veil of the unseen. You are invited to believe in it and act accordingly. On the Day when it becomes visible, belief will no longer benefit those who denied it. The time to avoid it is now, through faith and good deeds.\n\nThe verse also reminds us that the wicked will have no one to blame but themselves. They were warned. They were given messengers. They were shown signs. They chose disbelief. Seeing the Fire and knowing they cannot avoid it is the fruit of their own choices.",
  "54": "\"We have surely set forth in this Quran every kind of lesson for people, but humankind is the most argumentative of all beings\" reflects on the Quran's variety of teachings and on the human tendency to argue. The previous verse described the terror of seeing the Fire. This verse steps back and comments on why people still do not believe despite all the lessons.\n\nThe phrase \"every kind of lesson\" means the Quran uses every form of teaching that human beings need: stories, parables, commands, prohibitions, logical arguments, promises, warnings, descriptions of nature, and accounts of earlier nations. It does not repeat one style. It addresses the mind, the heart, and the conscience in ways that reach different kinds of people.\n\nThe word for lesson, \"mathal,\" also means parable and example. The Quran paints word pictures that make abstract truths concrete. The parable of the two gardens, the parable of the worldly life as plants, the story of the people of the cave, and the story of Moses and the servant of God are all examples of these lessons.\n\nDespite all this, humankind is \"the most argumentative of all beings.\" People argue about the truth even when it is clear. They debate with the messengers, raise objections, demand signs, and find reasons to reject what they know to be true. No amount of evidence satisfies a heart that has decided to disbelieve.\n\nThe phrase does not mean that argument is always wrong. Islam encourages thoughtful questions and sincere discussion. But it condemns argument for the sake of argument, the kind that uses falsehood to defeat truth and that resists clear evidence out of pride.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to be different. When you read the Quran, approach it with a heart that wants to learn, not a mind that wants to argue. The lessons are there, and they are sufficient. The question is whether you will receive them with submission or meet them with debate.\n\nThe most argumentative being is the one who has the most capacity to understand yet chooses to resist. Do not be that person. Let the Quran teach you rather than trying to teach it why it is wrong.",
  "55": "\"And nothing prevents people from believing when guidance comes to them and from seeking their Lord's forgiveness except their demand to meet the same fate of earlier deniers or that the torment would confront them face to face\" explains what actually prevents people from believing. The previous verse said human beings are argumentative. This verse identifies the root of that argumentativeness.\n\nThe verse says that nothing truly prevents people from faith except two demands. The first is that they demand the same fate as earlier deniers, meaning they want to see the punishment that was sent to previous nations before they will believe. The second is that they want the torment to come to them face to face, openly and directly.\n\nThese are not the demands of sincere seekers. A sincere person asks, \"What is the truth?\" The denier asks, \"Why does the punishment not come?\" They are not looking for evidence. They are challenging God, daring Him to punish them. This is the language of arrogance, not inquiry.\n\nThe phrase \"when guidance comes to them\" makes clear that the guidance has already arrived. The Quran is with them, the Prophet is among them, the signs are all around them. The obstacle is not that they have not heard. It is that they want the punishment as proof, which is a dangerous and foolish demand.\n\nThis demand has a long history. The people of Noah asked for the punishment. The people of Hud, Salih, Lot, and Shuaib challenged their messengers to bring what was threatened. In each case, the punishment came, and belief at that moment was too late.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning against treating the warnings of God as a challenge. If you find yourself saying, \"If God is real, let Him punish me,\" or \"I will believe when I see the angels,\" recognize this as the same arrogance that destroyed earlier nations.\n\nBelieve now, while the door is open and faith is of benefit. Do not wait until the torment is face to face. At that point, belief will not help the one who spent a lifetime denying.",
  "56": "\"We do not send the messengers except as deliverers of good news and warners. But the disbelievers argue in falsehood, hoping to discredit the truth with it, and make a mockery of My revelations and warnings\" defines the role of the messengers and describes how disbelievers respond. The previous verse said people demand punishment before believing. This verse explains what the messengers were actually sent to do.\n\nThe messengers are sent for two purposes: to deliver good news and to warn. The good news is God's mercy, Paradise, and reward for those who believe and do good. The warning is God's punishment, Hell, and accountability for those who reject truth and oppress others. There is no third role. They are not sent to force people to believe, to produce miracles on demand, or to run people's lives.\n\nThe disbelievers, however, respond with argument in falsehood. They do not engage with the message honestly. They use false reasoning, personal attacks, misrepresentations, and endless objections to discredit the truth. Their goal is not understanding. It is defeating the messenger.\n\nThe phrase \"hoping to discredit the truth with it\" shows the intention behind their arguments. They know the truth has power, so they try to undermine it through false debate. They may win an argument in the eyes of a crowd, but they do not change the truth.\n\nWorse still, they make a mockery of God's revelations and warnings. They laugh at the Quran, scoff at the Hereafter, and treat the divine message as a joke. Mockery is a defense mechanism. By laughing at the truth, they avoid having to take it seriously.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to recognize the difference between sincere questioning and mockery. It is normal to have questions. The Quran itself invites reflection. But if your questions are designed to discredit rather than understand, or if you treat serious things as jokes, you are following the way of the disbelievers described here.\n\nThe messengers delivered what they were sent to deliver. Whether you respond with good news or warning is up to you. But do not mistake argument for wisdom or mockery for freedom.",
  "57": "\"And who does more wrong than those who, when reminded of their Lord's revelations, turn away from them and forget what their own hands have done? We have certainly cast veils over their hearts—leaving them unable to comprehend this Quran—and deafness in their ears. And if you O Prophet invite them to true guidance, they will never be rightly guided\" describes the worst form of wrongdoing: turning away from reminder and forgetting one's own deeds.\n\nThe verse asks who is more wrong than the person who, when reminded of God's revelations, turns away. The word \"reminded\" implies that the truth has been brought to their attention. They have heard it. They know it. But they deliberately turn their backs on it.\n\nThey \"forget what their own hands have done,\" meaning they forget the sins they have committed. They do not learn from their mistakes. They do not reflect on the consequences of their actions. They move from one sin to the next without pausing to remember that they will be held accountable.\n\nThe result is that God casts veils over their hearts. As discussed earlier in the tafsir, this is both a divine act and a natural consequence of repeated rejection. The person who turns away from truth enough times finds that their heart can no longer understand it. The veils are not placed on an innocent seeker. They are placed on someone who has chosen to turn away.\n\nThe deafness in their ears is the same. The words of the Quran reach their physical ears, but they do not hear them in a way that leads to understanding. The message becomes noise, not guidance.\n\nThe final statement is sobering: even if the Prophet invites them, they will never be guided. This is not because God is unjust. It is because their hearts have reached a state where guidance cannot enter. The door that they repeatedly shut has finally closed.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a powerful warning about the danger of turning away from the Quran. Every time you hear the truth and ignore it, your heart grows a little harder. Do not let repeated rejection seal your heart. Respond to the reminder while your heart is still soft enough to respond.",
  "58": "\"Your Lord is the All-Forgiving, Full of Mercy. If He were to seize them immediately for what they commit, He would have certainly hastened their punishment. But they have an appointed time, from which they will find no refuge\" balances the severe warning of the previous verse with a reminder of God's forbearance. The previous verse described hearts that cannot be guided. This verse explains why God does not punish such people immediately.\n\nThe two names of God at the beginning, All-Forgiving and Full of Mercy, establish that God's default posture toward humanity is mercy, not punishment. He forgives those who turn to Him, and His mercy encompasses all things. Even the disbelievers are fed, clothed, and given time, because of His mercy.\n\nIf God were to punish people immediately for what they commit, no one would survive. Every person sins. Every person falls short. If punishment came the moment a sin was committed, human life would be destroyed. God's delay is not weakness. It is mercy, giving people time to repent.\n\nBut the verse then makes clear that the delay is not permanent. They have an \"appointed time,\" a fixed term known to God. When that time arrives, there will be no refuge. The punishment they mocked and the accounting they denied will come, and they will not escape it.\n\nThe phrase \"from which they will find no refuge\" means there will be no place to hide, no protector to turn to, and no way to flee. The only escape from punishment is repentance before the appointed time arrives. Once it arrives, repentance is too late.\n\nThis verse is one of the most balanced descriptions of God in the Quran. He is both All-Forgiving and Just. He delays punishment out of mercy, but He does not cancel it for those who die in disbelief. The delay is an invitation to repent, not evidence that sin does not matter.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is both a comfort and a warning. It is a comfort because God's mercy is vast and His patience is great. It is a warning because that patience has a limit. If you have sinned, turn back now, while the All-Forgiving is still forgiving and before the appointed time arrives.",
  "59": "\"Those are the societies We destroyed when they persisted in wrong, and We had set a time for their destruction\" points to the ruins of earlier nations as evidence of the appointed time mentioned in the previous verse. The previous verse said every person has an appointed time. This verse says that societies also have an appointed time.\n\nThe word \"those\" refers to the nations that were destroyed before: the people of Noah, Hud, Salih, Lot, Shuaib, Pharaoh, and others. They were societies that persisted in wrongdoing, rejected their messengers, and spread corruption. When their appointed time arrived, they were destroyed.\n\nThe phrase \"when they persisted in wrong\" is important. God did not destroy these societies after one sin. He sent messengers, gave them time, and waited. But when they persisted, when they made wrongdoing their settled way of life and refused every invitation to repent, destruction came.\n\nThe statement that God had set a time for their destruction means that it was not random. Each nation had a fixed term known to God. When that term was reached, no power could delay the punishment, just as no power could bring it before its time. The timing of divine judgment is precise.\n\nThe Quran often invites people to travel through the earth and see the ruins of earlier nations. Those ruins are signs. They are not ancient history with no meaning. They are evidence that the pattern described in the Quran is real and that every society that follows their path will meet their end.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning to societies as well as individuals. Nations are not exempt from God's moral law. They may rise, prosper, and appear invincible, but if they persist in wrongdoing, they too have an appointed time. The ruins of the past are a witness to this truth.\n\nIt is also a comfort to the oppressed. The tyrants who seem to escape justice in this life have an appointed time. They will not escape forever. The God who destroyed nations more powerful than they are has set a time for them as well.",
  "60": "“And ˹remember˺ when Moses said to his young assistant, “I will never give up until I reach the junction of the two seas, even if I travel for ages.”” opens the story of Moses and al-Khidr, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the vow: “And remember when Moses said to his young assistant, ‘I will never give up until I reach the junction of the two seas, even if I travel for ages,’” or “wa ith qala Moosa li-fatahu la abrahu hatta ablugha majma‘a al-bahrayni aw amdiya huquban,” and the commentators note the vow, for Moses vowed to journey until the junction of the two seas, and the vow is the resolve, the determination to seek the knowledge, and the commentators note the occasion, for Moses was asked about the most knowledgeable of the people, and he answered that he was, and the Lord revealed to him the servant at the junction of the two seas, and the verse is the record of the vow, the opening of the journey. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the junction of the two seas is the meeting place, and the vow is the resolve, and the verse is the record of the vow, the opening of the story.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the resolve, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own seeking. The believer is taught that the seeking of the knowledge is the duty, and the one who resolves to seek is the one whose journey the Lord guides, and the believer who travels for the knowledge is the believer whose effort the Lord rewards, and the vow of Moses is the model, the resolve that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the vow is the believer whose seeking the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the resolve of the seeker: Moses vowed the journey, and the believer who seeks the knowledge is the believer whose resolve the Lord values, and the journey is the teaching that the knowledge is the thing that the believer must seek. The verse is the record of the vow, and the teaching of the resolve that the seeking requires.",
  "61": "“But when they ˹finally˺ reached the point where the seas met, they forgot their ˹salted˺ fish, and it made its way into the sea, slipping away ˹wondrously˺.”” records the forgetting, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the arrival: “when they finally reached the point where the seas met, they forgot their salted fish,” or “fa-lamma balagha majma‘a baynihima nasiya hootahuma,” and the commentators note the arrival, for Moses and the assistant reached the junction and forgot the fish, and the forgetting is the sign, the beginning of the wonder, and the verse is the record of the arrival, the step that the following verses unfold. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the fish was the provision, and the forgetting is the sign, and the verse is the record of the arrival, the sign of the meeting. The second part is the slipping: “and it made its way into the sea, slipping away wondrously,” or “fattakhatha sabeelahu fi al-bahri saraban,” and the commentators note the slipping, for the fish took its way into the sea, and the slipping is the miracle, the sign that the Lord had arranged, and the verse is the record of the slipping, the wonder that the following verses recall.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own attention. The believer is taught that the signs of the Lord are the things that the believer must notice, and the one who attends to the sign is the one whose journey the Lord guides, and the believer who reads the signs is the believer whose attention the Lord values, and the slipping of the fish is the teaching, the declaration that the sign is the hidden, and the believer who reads the sign is the believer whose attention the wonder sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the attention of the seeker: the fish slipped, and the believer who attends to the signs is the believer whose journey the Lord guides, and the slipping is the sign that the believer must notice. The verse is the record of the forgetting, and the teaching of the sign that the seeker must notice.",
  "62": "“When they had passed further, he said to his assistant, “Bring us our meal! We have certainly been exhausted by today’s journey.”” records the request, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the passage: “When they had passed further, he said to his assistant,” or “fa-lamma jawaza qala li-fatahu,” and the commentators note the passage, for the two passed beyond the junction, and the verse is the record of the passage, the step that the following verse explains. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the passage is the distance, and the distance is the journey, and the verse is the record of the passage, the step of the story. The second part is the request: “Bring us our meal! We have certainly been exhausted by today’s journey,” or “atina ghada’ana laqad laqeena min safarina hatha nasaban,” and the commentators note the request, for Moses asked the meal, and the request is the occasion, the moment that the assistant remembered the fish, and the verse is the record of the request, the step that the following verse answers with the memory.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the journey, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own endurance. The believer is taught that the journey of the seeking is the toil, and the one who endures the toil is the one whose journey the Lord rewards, and the believer who travels for the knowledge is the believer whose fatigue the Lord values, and the request of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the seeking is the effort, and the believer who reads the request is the believer whose endurance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the toil of the seeking: Moses was exhausted, and the believer who seeks the knowledge is the believer whose toil the Lord rewards, and the fatigue is the measure of the effort that the seeking requires. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the endurance that the seeking demands.",
  "63": "“He replied, “Do you remember when we rested by the rock? ˹That is when˺ I forgot the fish. None made me forget to mention this except Satan. And the fish made its way into the sea miraculously.”” records the memory, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The first is the memory: “Do you remember when we rested by the rock? That is when I forgot the fish,” or “qala a-ra’ayta ith awayna ila as-sakhrati fa-innee naseetu al-hoota,” and the commentators note the memory, for the assistant remembered the fish at the rock, and the memory is the explanation, and the verse is the record of the memory, the explanation of the forgetting. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rock is the place, and the memory is the explanation, and the verse is the record of the memory, the explanation of the wonder. The second part is the attribution and the wonder: “None made me forget to mention this except Satan. And the fish made its way into the sea miraculously,” or “wa ma ansaneehu illa ash-shaytanu an athkurahu wattakhatha sabeelahu fi al-bahri ‘ajaban,” and the commentators note the attribution, for the assistant attributed the forgetting to Satan, and the wonder of the fish, and the verse is the record of the reply, the explanation that the following verse answers with the return.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the memory, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own attention. The believer is taught that the forgetting is the slip, and the one who forgets the sign is the one whose slip the Lord repairs, and the believer who remembers the sign is the believer whose attention the Lord values, and the reply of the assistant is the teaching, the declaration that the sign was the fish, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose attention the wonder sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the repair of the slip: the assistant remembered and explained, and the believer who forgets the sign is the believer whose slip the Lord guides, and the wonder of the fish is the sign that the believer must follow. The verse is the record of the memory, and the teaching of the sign that the seeker must follow.",
  "64": "“Moses responded, “That is ˹exactly˺ what we were looking for.” So they returned, retracing their footsteps.”” records the return, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the recognition: “Moses responded, ‘That is exactly what we were looking for,’” or “qala thalika ma kunna nabghi,” and the commentators note the recognition, for Moses recognized the sign, and the recognition is the key, the understanding that the fish was the sign of the meeting, and the verse is the record of the recognition, the step that the following clause completes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the sign is the fish, and the recognition is the key, and the verse is the record of the recognition, the understanding of the wonder. The second part is the return: “So they returned, retracing their footsteps,” or “fartadda ‘ala atharihima qasasan,” and the commentators note the return, for the two retraced the footsteps, and the return is the obedience, the seeking of the servant, and the verse is the record of the return, the step that the following verse fulfills with the meeting.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the recognition, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own seeking. The believer is taught that the recognition of the sign is the key, and the one who reads the sign aright is the one whose seeking the Lord guides, and the believer who retraces the footsteps is the believer whose return the Lord blesses, and the recognition of Moses is the model, the understanding that the believer must seek, and the believer who reads the recognition is the believer whose attention the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reading of the sign: Moses recognized and returned, and the believer who reads the signs is the believer whose journey the Lord guides, and the return is the step of the meeting. The verse is the record of the return, and the teaching of the recognition that the seeking requires.",
  "65": "“There they found a servant of Ours, to whom We had granted mercy from Us and enlightened with knowledge of Our Own.”” records the meeting, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the finding: “There they found a servant of Ours,” or “fa-wajada ‘abdan min ‘ibadina,” and the commentators note the finding, for the two found the servant, and the commentators identify the servant as al-Khidr, and the verse is the record of the finding, the meeting that the journey had sought. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the servant is al-Khidr, and the finding is the meeting, and the verse is the record of the finding, the meeting of Moses and the servant. The second part is the description: “to whom We had granted mercy from Us and enlightened with knowledge of Our Own,” or “ataynahu rahmatan min ‘indina wa ‘allamnahu min ladunna ‘ilman,” and the commentators note the description, for the servant was granted the mercy and the knowledge, and the description is the credentials, the declaration that the knowledge of the servant is the gift, and the verse is the record of the description, the credentials that the following verse confirms.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the knowledge, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own humility. The believer is taught that the knowledge is the gift, and the one who is given the knowledge is the one whose knowledge the Lord grants, and the believer who seeks the knowledge is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the description of the servant is the teaching, the declaration that the knowledge is the \"ladunni,\" the gift from the Lord, and the believer who reads the description is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens, for the knowledge of the servant is the gift, not the achievement. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the source of the knowledge: the servant was granted the knowledge, and the believer who seeks the knowledge is the believer whose humility the Lord accepts, and the gift is the thing that the believer must acknowledge. The verse is the record of the meeting, and the teaching of the knowledge that the Lord grants.",
  "66": "“Moses said to him, “May I follow you, provided that you teach me some of the right guidance you have been taught?”” records the request, and the commentators note the two parts of the request. The verse records the request: “May I follow you, provided that you teach me some of the right guidance you have been taught?” or “qala lahu Moosa hal attabi‘uka ‘ala an tu‘allimani mimma ‘ullimta rushdan,” and the commentators note the request, for Moses asked to follow the servant, and the request is the humility, the seeking of the knowledge, and the word \"rushd\" — the right guidance — is the object, and the verse is the record of the request, the humility that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the request of Moses is the model of the seeker, the humility of the one who asks the knowledge, and the verse is the record of the request, the humility of the prophet. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the request is the humility, and the humility is the key, and the verse is the record of the request, the humility of the seeker.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the humility, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own seeking. The believer is taught that the seeking of the knowledge is the humility, and the one who asks the knowledge is the one whose humility the Lord values, and the believer who follows the teacher is the believer whose humility the Lord rewards, and the request of Moses is the model, the humility of the prophet, and the believer who reads the request is the believer whose humility the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humility of the seeker: Moses asked to follow, and the believer who seeks the knowledge is the believer whose humility the Lord accepts, and the request is the key of the learning. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the humility that the seeking requires.",
  "67": "“He said, “You certainly cannot be patient ˹enough˺ with me.”” records the first reply, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the reply: “You certainly cannot be patient enough with me,” or “qala innaka lan tastatee‘a ma‘iya sabran,” and the commentators note the reply, for the servant warned Moses of the inability of the patience, and the warning is the condition, the declaration that the knowledge of the servant is the thing that the patience of Moses cannot bear, and the verse is the record of the reply, the first half of the condition that the following verse explains. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the warning is the condition, and the condition is the truth, and the verse is the record of the reply, the warning that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the patience, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own endurance. The believer is taught that the knowledge of the hidden is the thing that the patience must bear, and the one who seeks the knowledge is the one whose patience the Lord tests, and the believer who is warned of the patience is the believer whose endurance the Lord values, and the reply of the servant is the teaching, the declaration that the patience is the condition, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose endurance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the condition of the patience: the servant warned of the inability, and the believer who seeks the knowledge is the believer whose patience the Lord tests, and the warning is the teaching that the patience is the key. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the patience that the knowledge demands.",
  "68": "“And how can you be patient with what is beyond your ˹realm of˺ knowledge?”” completes the warning, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the warning: “And how can you be patient with what is beyond your realm of knowledge?” or “wa kayfa tasbiru ‘ala ma lam tuhit bihi khubran,” and the commentators note the question, for the servant explained the reason of the warning, and the reason is the knowledge, the declaration that the patience is the thing that the unknown cannot bear, and the verse is the record of the completion, the explanation that the following verse answers with the assurance of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the unknown is the reason, and the reason is the truth, and the verse is the record of the completion, the explanation of the condition.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the unknown, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the unknown is the thing that the patience must bear, and the one who trusts the Lord in the unknown is the one whose patience the Lord strengthens, and the believer who follows the teacher is the believer whose trust the Lord tests, and the explanation of the servant is the teaching, the declaration that the knowledge is the hidden, and the believer who reads the explanation is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the unknown: the servant explained the reason, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose patience the Lord confirms, and the explanation is the teaching that the unknown is the test. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the trust that the unknown requires.",
  "69": "“Moses assured ˹him˺, “You will find me patient, Allah willing, and I will not disobey any of your orders.”” records the assurance, and the commentators note the two parts of the assurance. The first is the patience: “You will find me patient, Allah willing,” or “qala satajidunee in sha’a Allahu sabiran,” and the commentators note the assurance, for Moses promised the patience, and the exception of the will is the reliance, the attachment of the promise to the Lord, and the verse is the record of the assurance, the promise that the following clause completes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the exception is the reliance, and the promise is the resolve, and the verse is the record of the assurance, the promise of the patience. The second part is the obedience: “and I will not disobey any of your orders,” or “wa la a‘see laka amran,” and the commentators note the obedience, for Moses promised the obedience, and the verse is the record of the assurance, the promise that the following verse qualifies with the condition.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the exception, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The believer is taught that the promise is the resolve, and the exception of the will is the reliance, and the one who says \"if Allah wills\" is the one whose promise the Lord blesses, and the believer who promises with the exception is the believer whose reliance the Lord values, and the assurance of Moses is the model, the promise that the believer must make with the exception, and the believer who reads the assurance is the believer whose reliance the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reliance of the promise: Moses promised with the exception, and the believer who promises with the will is the believer whose promise the Lord accepts, and the exception is the key of the reliance. The verse is the record of the assurance, and the teaching of the exception that the promise requires.",
  "70": "“He responded, “Then if you follow me, do not question me about anything until I ˹myself˺ clarify it for you.”” records the condition, and the commentators note the two parts of the condition. The verse records the condition: “Then if you follow me, do not question me about anything until I myself clarify it for you,” or “qala fa-ini ittaba‘tanee fa-la tas’alnee ‘an shay’in hatta uhditha laka minhu thikran,” and the commentators note the condition, for the servant set the condition of the silence, and the condition is the covenant, the declaration that Moses must not question until the clarification, and the verse is the record of the condition, the covenant that the following verses test, and the commentators note the significance, for the condition is the test, and the test is the patience, and the verse is the record of the condition, the covenant that the story unfolds. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the condition is the covenant, and the covenant is the test, and the verse is the record of the condition, the covenant of the silence.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the condition, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the following of the teacher is the covenant, and the one who accepts the condition is the one whose patience the Lord tests, and the believer who follows with the silence is the believer whose endurance the Lord values, and the condition of the servant is the teaching, the declaration that the patience is the key, and the believer who reads the condition is the believer whose endurance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the covenant of the learning: the servant set the condition, and the believer who follows the teacher is the believer whose patience the Lord tests, and the condition is the teaching that the silence is the key. The verse is the record of the condition, and the teaching of the patience that the covenant requires.",
  "71": "“So they set out, but after they had boarded a ship, the man made a hole in it. Moses protested, “Have you done this to drown its people? You have certainly done a terrible thing!”” records the first act, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the act: “So they set out, but after they had boarded a ship, the man made a hole in it,” or “fantalaqa hatta itha rakiba fi as-safeenati kharaqaha,” and the commentators note the act, for the servant made a hole in the ship, and the act is the first of the three, and the verse is the record of the act, the test that the following clause describes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the hole is the first act, and the act is the test, and the verse is the record of the act, the first of the three tests. The second part is the protest: “Moses protested, ‘Have you done this to drown its people? You have certainly done a terrible thing!’” or “qala a-kharaqtaha li-tughriqa ahlaha laqad ji’ta shay’an imran,” and the commentators note the protest, for Moses protested the hole, and the protest is the first question, the breaking of the covenant, and the verse is the record of the protest, the question that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the protest, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the patience is the test, and the one who questions before the clarification is the one whose covenant the question breaks, and the believer who holds the silence is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the protest of Moses is the warning, the declaration that the question before the time is the slip, and the believer who reads the protest is the believer whose endurance the warning sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the test of the patience: Moses protested, and the believer who follows the teacher is the believer whose patience the Lord tests, and the protest is the first slip that the covenant would weigh. The verse is the record of the first act, and the teaching of the patience that the test demands.",
  "72": "“He replied, “Did I not say that you cannot have patience with me?”” records the first reminder, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the reminder: “Did I not say that you cannot have patience with me?” or “qala a-lam aqul innaka lan tastatee‘a ma‘iya sabran,” and the commentators note the reminder, for the servant reminded Moses of the warning, and the reminder is the gentle reproof, the declaration that the slip was the thing that the warning had foretold, and the verse is the record of the reminder, the reproof that the following verse answers with the plea. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reminder is the reproof, and the reproof is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the reminder, the gentle correction of the slip.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reminder, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own correction. The believer is taught that the reminder of the slip is the correction, and the one who is reminded is the one whose correction the Lord values, and the believer who accepts the reproof is the believer whose humility the Lord rewards, and the reminder of the servant is the model, the gentle reproof that the believer must accept, and the believer who reads the reminder is the believer whose humility the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the acceptance of the correction: Moses was reminded, and the believer who accepts the reproof is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the reminder is the teaching that the slip is the thing that the correction repairs. The verse is the record of the reminder, and the teaching of the humility that the correction requires.",
  "73": "“Moses pleaded, “Excuse me for forgetting, and do not be hard on me.”” records the plea, and the commentators note the two parts of the plea. The verse records the plea: “Excuse me for forgetting, and do not be hard on me,” or “qala la tu’akhithnee bima naseetu wa la turhiqnee min amree ‘usran,” and the commentators note the plea, for Moses asked the excuse for the forgetting, and the plea is the repentance, the acknowledgment of the slip, and the verse is the record of the plea, the humility that the following verse answers with the continuation, and the commentators note the significance, for the plea of Moses is the model of the repentance, the asking of the excuse and the sparing of the hardship. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the plea is the repentance, and the repentance is the humility, and the verse is the record of the plea, the humility of the prophet.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plea, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own repentance. The believer is taught that the slip is repaired by the plea, and the one who asks the excuse is the one whose repentance the Lord values, and the believer who confesses the forgetfulness is the believer whose humility the Lord rewards, and the plea of Moses is the model, the repentance of the prophet, and the believer who reads the plea is the believer whose humility the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the repair of the slip: Moses pleaded, and the believer who slips is the believer whose plea the Lord accepts, and the plea is the key of the continuation. The verse is the record of the plea, and the teaching of the repentance that the slip requires.",
  "74": "“So they proceeded until they came across a boy, and the man killed him. Moses protested, “Have you killed an innocent soul, who killed no one? You have certainly done a horrible thing.”” records the second act, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the act: “So they proceeded until they came across a boy, and the man killed him,” or “fantalaqa hatta itha laqiya ghulaman fa-qatalahu,” and the commentators note the act, for the servant killed the boy, and the act is the second of the three, and the verse is the record of the act, the test that the following clause describes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the killing is the second act, and the act is the test, and the verse is the record of the act, the second of the three tests. The second part is the protest: “Moses protested, ‘Have you killed an innocent soul, who killed no one? You have certainly done a horrible thing!’” or “qala a-qatalta nafsan zakiyyatan bi-ghayri nafsin laqad ji’ta shay’an nukran,” and the commentators note the protest, for Moses protested the killing, and the protest is the second question, and the verse is the record of the protest, the question that the following verse answers with the reminder.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the protest, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the patience is the test, and the one who questions the hidden act is the one whose covenant the question breaks, and the believer who holds the silence is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the protest of Moses is the warning, the declaration that the question before the clarification is the slip, and the believer who reads the protest is the believer whose endurance the warning sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the test of the patience: Moses protested again, and the believer who follows the teacher is the believer whose patience the Lord tests, and the protest is the second slip that the covenant would weigh. The verse is the record of the second act, and the teaching of the patience that the test demands.",
  "75": "“He answered, “Did I not tell you that you cannot have patience with me?”” records the second reminder, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the reminder: “Did I not tell you that you cannot have patience with me?” or “qala a-lam aqul laka innaka lan tastatee‘a ma‘iya sabran,” and the commentators note the reminder, for the servant reminded Moses of the warning, and the reminder is the second reproof, and the verse is the record of the reminder, the reproof that the following verse answers with the final plea, and the commentators note the significance, for the repetition of the reminder is the teaching, the declaration that the patience is the thing that Moses had not yet held. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reminder is the reproof, and the repetition is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the reminder, the second correction.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reminder, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own correction. The believer is taught that the repetition of the reminder is the correction, and the one who is reminded twice is the one whose correction the Lord deepens, and the believer who accepts the reproof is the believer whose humility the Lord rewards, and the reminder of the servant is the model, the gentle reproof that the believer must accept, and the believer who reads the reminder is the believer whose humility the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the acceptance of the correction: Moses was reminded again, and the believer who accepts the reproof is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the repetition is the teaching that the slip is the thing that the correction repairs. The verse is the record of the reminder, and the teaching of the humility that the correction requires.",
  "76": "“Moses replied, “If I ever question you about anything after this, then do not keep me in your company, for by then I would have given you enough of an excuse.”” records the final pledge, and the commentators note the two parts of the pledge. The verse records the pledge: “If I ever question you about anything after this, then do not keep me in your company, for by then I would have given you enough of an excuse,” or “qala in sa’altuka ‘an shay’in ba‘daha fa-la tusahibnee qad balaghta min ladunnee ‘uthran,” and the commentators note the pledge, for Moses pledged the silence, and the pledge is the resolve, the declaration that the next question would end the company, and the verse is the record of the pledge, the resolve that the following verse tests with the third act. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the pledge is the resolve, and the resolve is the humility, and the verse is the record of the pledge, the final promise of Moses.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the pledge, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own resolve. The believer is taught that the pledge is the resolve, and the one who pledges the patience is the one whose resolve the Lord tests, and the believer who holds the silence is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the pledge of Moses is the model, the resolve that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the pledge is the believer whose endurance the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the resolve of the patience: Moses pledged, and the believer who holds the patience is the believer whose resolve the Lord tests, and the pledge is the teaching that the silence is the key. The verse is the record of the pledge, and the teaching of the resolve that the patience requires.",
  "77": "“So they moved on until they came to the people of a town. They asked them for food, but the people refused to give them hospitality. There they found a wall ready to collapse, so the man set it right. Moses protested, “If you wanted, you could have demanded a fee for this.”” records the third act, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the act: “So they moved on until they came to the people of a town… There they found a wall ready to collapse, so the man set it right,” or “fantalaqa hatta itha ataya ahla qaryatin istat‘ama ahlaha fa-abaw an yudayyifoohuma fa-wajada feeha jidaran yureedu an yanqadda fa-aqamahu,” and the commentators note the act, for the servant set the wall right, and the act is the third of the three, and the verse is the record of the act, the test that the following clause describes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the wall is the third act, and the act is the test, and the verse is the record of the act, the third of the three tests. The second part is the protest: “Moses protested, ‘If you wanted, you could have demanded a fee for this,’” or “qala law shi’ta lattakhathta ‘alayhi ajran,” and the commentators note the protest, for Moses protested the free service, and the protest is the third question, the one that the pledge had warned would end the company, and the verse is the record of the protest, the question that the following verse answers with the parting.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the protest, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the patience is the test, and the one who questions the hidden act is the one whose covenant the question breaks, and the believer who holds the silence is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the protest of Moses is the warning, the declaration that the third question is the end, and the believer who reads the protest is the believer whose endurance the warning sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the test of the patience: Moses protested the third time, and the believer who follows the teacher is the believer whose patience the Lord tests, and the protest is the end of the company. The verse is the record of the third act, and the teaching of the patience that the test demands.",
  "78": "“He replied, “This is the parting of our ways. I will explain to you what you could not bear patiently.”” records the parting, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the parting: “This is the parting of our ways. I will explain to you what you could not bear patiently,” or “qala hatha firaqu baynee wa baynika sa-unabbi’uka bi-ta’weeli ma lam tastati‘ ‘alayhi sabran,” and the commentators note the parting, for the servant declared the parting and promised the explanation, and the parting is the fulfillment of the pledge, and the explanation is the mercy, the teaching that the following verses unfold, and the verse is the record of the parting, the step that the following verses complete, and the commentators note the significance, for the parting is the end of the journey, and the explanation is the beginning of the understanding. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the parting is the condition, and the explanation is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the parting, the teaching that the following verses deliver.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the parting, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own understanding. The believer is taught that the parting is the consequence of the question, and the one who breaks the covenant is the one whose company ends, and the believer who holds the patience is the believer whose understanding the Lord grants, and the parting of the servant is the teaching, the declaration that the patience is the condition of the knowledge, and the believer who reads the parting is the believer whose endurance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the consequence of the impatience: Moses parted, and the believer who holds the patience is the believer whose company the Lord preserves, and the explanation is the mercy that the parting introduced. The verse is the record of the parting, and the teaching of the patience that the knowledge requires.",
  "79": "““As for the ship, it belonged to some poor people, working at sea. So I intended to damage it, for there was a ˹tyrant˺ king ahead of them who seizes every ˹good˺ ship by force.”” records the first explanation, and the commentators note the two parts of the explanation. The verse records the explanation: “As for the ship, it belonged to some poor people, working at sea. So I intended to damage it, for there was a tyrant king ahead of them who seizes every good ship by force,” or “amma as-safeenatu fa-kanat li-masakeena ya‘maloona fi al-bahri fa-aradtu an a‘eebaha wa kana wara’ahum malikun ya’khuthu kulla safeenatin ghasban,” and the commentators note the explanation, for the hole was the mercy, the damage that saved the ship from the tyrant, and the explanation is the teaching, the declaration that the apparent harm was the hidden good, and the verse is the record of the explanation, the first of the three meanings. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the hole was the protection, and the protection is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the explanation, the first of the three wisdoms.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the hidden, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the apparent harm is sometimes the hidden good, and the one who trusts the Lord is the one whose trust the hidden justifies, and the believer who bears the apparent harm is the believer whose patience the Lord rewards, and the explanation of the servant is the teaching, the declaration that the wisdom is the hidden, and the believer who reads the explanation is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the hidden wisdom: the hole saved the ship, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the hidden confirms, and the explanation is the teaching that the harm is sometimes the mercy. The verse is the record of the first explanation, and the teaching of the trust that the hidden requires.",
  "80": "““And as for the boy, his parents were ˹true˺ believers, and we feared that he would pressure them into defiance and disbelief.”” records the second explanation, and the commentators note the two parts of the explanation. The verse records the explanation: “And as for the boy, his parents were true believers, and we feared that he would pressure them into defiance and disbelief,” or “wa amma al-ghulamu fa-kana abawahu mu’minayni fa-khasheena an yurhiqahuma tughyanan wa kufran,” and the commentators note the explanation, for the killing of the boy was the mercy, the prevention of the oppression and the disbelief, and the explanation is the teaching, the declaration that the apparent wrong was the hidden good, and the verse is the record of the explanation, the second of the three meanings. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the killing was the protection, and the protection is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the explanation, the second of the three wisdoms.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the hidden, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the apparent wrong is sometimes the hidden good, and the one who trusts the Lord is the one whose trust the hidden justifies, and the believer who bears the apparent wrong is the believer whose patience the Lord rewards, and the explanation of the servant is the teaching, the declaration that the wisdom is the hidden, and the believer who reads the explanation is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the hidden wisdom: the killing saved the parents, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the hidden confirms, and the explanation is the teaching that the wrong is sometimes the mercy. The verse is the record of the second explanation, and the teaching of the trust that the hidden requires.",
  "81": "“So we hoped that their Lord would give them another, more virtuous and caring in his place.”” completes the second explanation, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the explanation: “So we hoped that their Lord would give them another, more virtuous and caring in his place,” or “fa-aradna an yubdilahuma rabbuhuma khayran minhu zakatan wa aqraba ruhman,” and the commentators note the completion, for the Lord would give the parents the better son, and the completion is the teaching, the declaration that the replacement is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the completion, the hope that the parents received, and the commentators note the significance, for the replacement is the compensation, and the compensation is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the completion, the second wisdom. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the replacement is the mercy, and the mercy is the gift, and the verse is the record of the completion, the compensation that the parents received.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the replacement, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the removal is sometimes the mercy, and the one who is deprived is the one whom the Lord compensates, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the loss is the believer whose compensation the Lord grants, and the completion of the servant is the teaching, the declaration that the replacement is the better, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the compensation of the Lord: the son was replaced, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the loss is the believer whose compensation the Lord supplies, and the replacement is the mercy that the believer must trust. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the hope that the replacement grants.",
  "82": "““And as for the wall, it belonged to two orphan boys in the city, and under the wall was a treasure that belonged to them, and their father had been a righteous man. So your Lord willed that these children should come of age and retrieve their treasure, as a mercy from your Lord. I did not do it ˹all˺ on my own. This is the explanation of what you could not bear patiently.”” records the third explanation, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the explanation: “And as for the wall, it belonged to two orphan boys… and under the wall was a treasure that belonged to them, and their father had been a righteous man. So your Lord willed that these children should come of age and retrieve their treasure, as a mercy from your Lord,” or “wa amma al-jidaru fa-kana li-ghulamayni yateemayni fi al-madeenati wa kana tahtahu kanzun lahuma wa kana aboohuma salihan fa-arada rabbuka an yablugha ashuddahuma wa yastakhrija kanzahuma rahmatan min rabbika,” and the commentators note the explanation, for the wall was the protection of the treasure, and the explanation is the teaching, the declaration that the apparent free service was the hidden good, and the verse is the record of the explanation, the third of the three meanings. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the wall was the protection, and the protection is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the explanation, the third of the three wisdoms. The second part is the disclaimer: “I did not do it all on my own. This is the explanation of what you could not bear patiently,” or “wa ma fa‘altuhu ‘an amree thalika ta’weelu ma lam tastati‘ ‘alayhi sabran,” and the commentators note the disclaimer, for the servant attributed the acts to the Lord, and the disclaimer is the humility, the declaration that the wisdom is the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the completion, the explanation that the story closes with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the humility, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the hidden wisdom is the Lord's, and the one who attributes the wisdom to the Lord is the one whose humility the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the hidden is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the disclaimer of the servant is the teaching, the declaration that the acts are the Lord's, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens, and the three explanations are the teaching that the apparent is not the hidden, and the wisdom of the Lord is the thing that the believer must trust. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humility of the knowledge: the servant attributed the acts to the Lord, and the believer who trusts the hidden is the believer whose trust the Lord rewards, and the three explanations are the proof that the wisdom of the Lord is the hidden. The verse is the record of the third explanation, and the teaching of the trust that the hidden demands.",
  "83": "“They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ about Ⱬul-Qarnain. Say, “I will relate to you something of his narrative.”” opens the story of Dhul-Qarnayn, and the commentators note the occasion and the command. The verse records the question: “They ask you O Prophet about Dhul-Qarnain,” or “wa yas’aloonaka ‘an thi al-qarnayni,” and the commentators note the occasion, for the Quraysh, prompted by the Jews, asked the Prophet about Dhul-Qarnayn, and the question was the test, and the verse records the answer: “Say, ‘I will relate to you something of his narrative,’” or “qul sa-atloo ‘alaykum minhu thikran,” and the commentators note the word “minhu” — of him, some of — for the answer is the portion of the narrative, not the whole, and the limitation is the teaching, the declaration that the full knowledge of the man is the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the command, the opening of the story that the following verses unfold. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for Dhul-Qarnayn is the “one of the two horns,” the title of the great king, and the question of the Quraysh is the question that the revelation answered, and the verse is the record of the command, the introduction of the narrative.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the narrative, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reflection. The believer is taught that the story of Dhul-Qarnayn is the portion, and the full knowledge is the Lord's, and the one who reads the narrative is the one whose lesson the story delivers, and the believer who reflects upon the king and his justice is the believer whose reflection the story guides, and the verse is the invitation, the call to the reflection upon the journey and the barrier and the justice. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the manner of the narrative: the portion is related, and the believer who reads the portion is the believer who extracts the lesson, and the limitation is the humility that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the reflection that the story invites.",
  "84": "“Surely We established him in the land, and gave him the means to all things.”” records the establishment, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse states the establishment: “Surely We established him in the land, and gave him the means to all things,” or “inna makkanna lahu fi al-ardi wa ataynahu min kulli shay’in sababan,” and the commentators note the two clauses, the “tamkeen,” the establishment, and the “asbab,” the means, and the verse is the declaration, the teaching that the power of Dhul-Qarnayn was the gift of the Lord, and the commentators note the significance, for the establishment and the means are the two things that the Lord granted, and the grant is the foundation of the justice that the story records. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the establishment is the dominion, and the means are the instruments, and the verse is the record of the establishment, the grant that the following verses display, and the commentators note that the “means to all things” is the knowledge and the provision and the power, the three that the journey required.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the grant, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own stewardship. The believer is taught that the power is the gift, and the one who is established is the one whose establishment the Lord tests, and the believer who is given the means is the believer whose means the Lord weighs, and the establishment of Dhul-Qarnayn is the teaching, the declaration that the dominion is the trust, and the believer who reads the establishment is the believer whose stewardship the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the source of the power: the establishment and the means are the Lord's, and the believer who is given the power is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the grant is the test that the following journey displays. The verse is the record of the establishment, and the teaching of the stewardship that the power requires.",
  "85": "“So he travelled a course,” records the first journey, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the journey: “So he travelled a course,” or “fa-atba‘a sababan,” and the commentators note the word “sabab,” the course, the route, the means, for Dhul-Qarnayn followed the course, and the journey is the first of the three, and the verse is the record of the journey, the step that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the journey of the king is the journey of the justice, the travel to the ends of the earth that the Lord had granted, and the verse is the record of the journey, the opening of the three courses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the course is the route, and the route is the means, and the verse is the record of the journey, the first of the three travels that the story records.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the journey, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own striving. The believer is taught that the journey of the believer is the journey of the duty, and the one who travels with the means is the one whose travel the Lord guides, and the believer who uses the means for the justice is the believer whose striving the Lord values, and the journey of Dhul-Qarnayn is the model, the travel of the king to the ends of the earth, and the believer who reads the journey is the believer whose striving the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the use of the means: the king travelled the course, and the believer who uses the means for the good is the believer whose travel the Lord blesses, and the journey is the teaching that the power is the instrument of the justice. The verse is the record of the journey, and the teaching of the striving that the means enables.",
  "86": "“until he reached the setting ˹point˺ of the sun, which appeared to him to be setting in a spring of murky water, where he found some people. We said, “O Ⱬul-Qarnain! Either punish them or treat them kindly.”” records the first destination, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the destination: “until he reached the setting point of the sun, which appeared to him to be setting in a spring of murky water, where he found some people,” or “hatta itha balagha maghriba ash-shamsi wajadaha taghrubu fee ‘aynin hami’atin wa wajada ‘indaha qawman,” and the commentators note the destination, for Dhul-Qarnayn reached the setting of the sun, and the commentators explain the phrase, for the sun appeared to him to be setting in the murky spring, the “hami’ah,” the black mud, and the appearance is the description of the horizon, and the people are the ones whom he found, and the verse is the record of the destination, the first of the three ends. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the setting of the sun is the western end, and the spring is the description of the sea, and the verse is the record of the destination, the first of the three reaches. The second part is the choice: “We said, ‘O Dhul-Qarnain! Either punish them or treat them kindly,’” or “qulna ya tha al-qarnayni imma an tu‘aththiba wa imma an tattakhitha feehim husnan,” and the commentators note the choice, for the Lord granted the king the choice between the punishment and the kindness, and the choice is the test, the declaration that the power is the trust, and the verse is the record of the choice, the test that the following verse answers with the justice.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the choice, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own judgment. The believer is taught that the power is the trust, and the one who is given the choice is the one whose judgment the Lord tests, and the believer who judges with the justice is the believer whose judgment the Lord values, and the choice of Dhul-Qarnayn is the teaching, the declaration that the power is the test, and the believer who reads the choice is the believer whose justice the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the responsibility of the power: the king was given the choice, and the believer who is given the power is the believer whose justice the Lord weighs, and the choice is the test that the following verse answers with the justice. The verse is the record of the destination and the choice, and the teaching of the justice that the power requires.",
  "87": "“He responded, “Whoever does wrong will be punished by us, then will be returned to their Lord, Who will punish them with a horrible torment.”” records the first half of the justice, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: “Whoever does wrong will be punished by us, then will be returned to their Lord, Who will punish them with a horrible torment,” or “qala amma man zalama fa-sawfa nu‘aththibuhu thumma yuraddu ila rabbihi fa-yu‘aththibuhu ‘athaban nukran,” and the commentators note the reply, for Dhul-Qarnayn declared the two punishments, the worldly punishment and the punishment of the Lord, and the reply is the justice, the declaration that the wrongdoer is punished in the world and in the next, and the verse is the record of the reply, the justice that the following verse completes with the reward. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the two punishments are the recompense, and the recompense is the justice, and the verse is the record of the reply, the first half of the justice of the king.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the justice, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own judgment. The believer is taught that the wrong is punished, and the one who wrongs is the one whom the punishment finds in both worlds, and the believer who judges with the justice is the believer whose judgment the Lord values, and the reply of Dhul-Qarnayn is the model, the declaration that the wrongdoer is punished, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose justice the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the two punishments: the king punished the wrong, and the believer who judges with the justice is the believer whose judgment the Lord confirms, and the reply is the teaching that the wrong is not left without the recompense. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the justice that the wrong demands.",
  "88": "“As for those who believe and do good, they will have the finest reward, and we will assign them easy commands.”” completes the justice, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the reply: “As for those who believe and do good, they will have the finest reward, and we will assign them easy commands,” or “wa amma man amana wa ‘amila salihan fa-lahu jaza’an al-husna wa sa-naqoolu lahu min amrina yusran,” and the commentators note the reply, for Dhul-Qarnayn declared the reward of the believers, and the reply is the justice, the declaration that the good-doer receives the finest reward and the easy command, and the verse is the record of the reply, the completion of the justice, and the commentators note the significance, for the king who punished the wrong rewarded the good, and the two together are the complete justice, and the verse is the record of the completion, the justice that the story upholds. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reward is the mercy, and the easy command is the kindness, and the verse is the record of the completion, the second half of the justice.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reward, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the good is rewarded, and the one who believes and does the good is the one whom the reward finds, and the believer who hopes the finest reward is the believer whose good the Lord values, and the reply of Dhul-Qarnayn is the model, the declaration that the good-doer is rewarded, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose hope the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the two halves of the justice: the king rewarded the good, and the believer who does the good is the believer whose reward the Lord grants, and the completion is the teaching that the justice is the balanced. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the justice that the good receives.",
  "89": "“Then he travelled a ˹different˺ course” records the second journey, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the journey: “Then he travelled a different course,” or “thumma atba‘a sababan,” and the commentators note the journey, for Dhul-Qarnayn followed the second course, and the journey is the second of the three, and the verse is the record of the journey, the step that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the second journey is the second end, the rising of the sun, and the verse is the record of the journey, the second of the three travels. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the course is the route, and the route is the second, and the verse is the record of the journey, the second travel that the story records.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the journey, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own striving. The believer is taught that the journey of the duty is the continuous, and the one who travels the courses is the one whose travel the Lord guides, and the believer who persists in the striving is the believer whose persistence the Lord values, and the journey of Dhul-Qarnayn is the model, the travel to the second end, and the believer who reads the journey is the believer whose striving the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the persistence of the striving: the king travelled the second course, and the believer who persists in the good is the believer whose travel the Lord blesses, and the journey is the teaching that the duty is the continuous. The verse is the record of the journey, and the teaching of the striving that the duty requires.",
  "90": "“until he reached the rising ˹point˺ of the sun. He found it rising on a people for whom We had provided no shelter from it.”” records the second destination, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the destination: “until he reached the rising point of the sun. He found it rising on a people for whom We had provided no shelter from it,” or “hatta itha balagha matli‘a ash-shamsi wajadaha tatlu‘u ‘ala qawmin lam naj‘al lahum min dooniha sitran,” and the commentators note the destination, for Dhul-Qarnayn reached the rising of the sun, and the people are the ones without the shelter, and the verse is the record of the destination, the second of the three ends, and the commentators note the significance, for the people without the shelter are the description of the eastern end, and the verse is the record of the destination, the second reach. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rising of the sun is the eastern end, and the people are the ones of the open land, and the verse is the record of the destination, the second of the three reaches.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the destination, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reflection. The believer is taught that the ends of the earth are the Lord's, and the one who reaches the ends is the one whose reach the Lord grants, and the believer who reflects upon the vastness is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the destination of Dhul-Qarnayn is the teaching, the declaration that the earth is the Lord's, and the believer who reads the destination is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the vastness of the earth: the king reached the rising, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose reflection the Lord guides, and the destination is the teaching that the earth is the expanse that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the destination, and the teaching of the reflection that the vastness invites.",
  "91": "“So it was. And We truly had full knowledge of him.”” records the knowledge, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse states the knowledge: “So it was. And We truly had full knowledge of him,” or “kathalika wa qad ahatna bima ladayhi khubran,” and the commentators note the two clauses, the confirmation and the knowledge, and the verse is the declaration, the teaching that the Lord knew the state of Dhul-Qarnayn, and the commentators note the significance, for the knowledge of the Lord is the assurance, the declaration that the journey and the justice were within the knowledge of the Lord, and the verse is the record of the knowledge, the assurance that the following journey continues. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the knowledge is the assurance, and the assurance is the comfort, and the verse is the record of the knowledge, the assurance that the Lord knew the king.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the knowledge, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the Lord knows the states of the servants, and the one who journeys is the one whose journey the Lord knows, and the believer who trusts the knowledge is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the knowledge of the Lord is the teaching, the declaration that the journey and the justice are the seen, and the believer who reads the knowledge is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the knowledge of the Lord: the Lord knew the king, and the believer who trusts the knowledge is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the knowledge is the assurance that the deeds are the seen. The verse is the record of the knowledge, and the teaching of the certainty that the knowledge produces.",
  "92": "“Then he travelled a ˹third˺ course” records the third journey, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the journey: “Then he travelled a third course,” or “thumma atba‘a sababan,” and the commentators note the journey, for Dhul-Qarnayn followed the third course, and the journey is the third of the three, and the verse is the record of the journey, the step that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the third journey is the pass between the two mountains, and the verse is the record of the journey, the third of the three travels. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the course is the route, and the route is the third, and the verse is the record of the journey, the third travel that the story records.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the journey, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own striving. The believer is taught that the journey of the duty is the threefold, and the one who travels the courses is the one whose travel the Lord guides, and the believer who completes the striving is the believer whose completion the Lord values, and the journey of Dhul-Qarnayn is the model, the travel to the third end, and the believer who reads the journey is the believer whose striving the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completion of the striving: the king travelled the third course, and the believer who completes the duty is the believer whose travel the Lord blesses, and the journey is the teaching that the duty is the complete. The verse is the record of the journey, and the teaching of the striving that the duty completes.",
  "93": "“until he reached ˹a pass˺ between two mountains. He found in front of them a people who could hardly understand ˹his˺ language.”” records the third destination, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the destination: “until he reached a pass between two mountains. He found in front of them a people who could hardly understand his language,” or “hatta itha balagha bayna as-saddayni wajada min doonihima qawman la yakadoona yafqahoona qawlan,” and the commentators note the destination, for Dhul-Qarnayn reached the pass between the two mountains, and the people are the ones who could hardly understand, and the verse is the record of the destination, the third of the three ends, and the commentators note the significance, for the pass between the mountains is the place of the barrier, and the people are the ones who sought the help, and the verse is the record of the destination, the third reach. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the two mountains are the sides of the pass, and the people are the ones of the frontier, and the verse is the record of the destination, the third of the three reaches.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the destination, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own service. The believer is taught that the destination of the journey is the service, and the one who reaches the people is the one whose help the Lord values, and the believer who serves the needy is the believer whose service the Lord rewards, and the destination of Dhul-Qarnayn is the teaching, the declaration that the power reaches the weak, and the believer who reads the destination is the believer whose service the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the service of the power: the king reached the people, and the believer who serves the weak is the believer whose service the Lord blesses, and the destination is the teaching that the power is the service. The verse is the record of the destination, and the teaching of the service that the power enables.",
  "94": "“They pleaded, “O Ⱬul-Qarnain! Surely Gog and Magog are spreading corruption throughout the land. Should we pay you tribute, provided that you build a wall between us and them?”” records the plea, and the commentators note the two parts of the plea. The verse records the plea: “O Dhul-Qarnain! Surely Gog and Magog are spreading corruption throughout the land. Should we pay you tribute, provided that you build a wall between us and them?” or “qaloo ya tha al-qarnayni inna Ya’jooja wa Ma’jooja mufsidoona fi al-ardi fa-hal naj‘alu laka kharjan ‘ala an taj‘ala baynana wa baynahum saddan,” and the commentators note the plea, for the people complained of the corruption of Gog and Magog, and the offer of the tribute, and the verse is the record of the plea, the request that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for Gog and Magog are the two corrupting peoples, and the plea is the appeal of the weak to the strong, and the verse is the record of the plea, the request of the barrier. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for Gog and Magog are the two nations of the corruption, and the plea is the appeal, and the verse is the record of the plea, the request that the king answered.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plea, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own help. The believer is taught that the plea of the weak is the duty of the strong, and the one who is able is the one whose help the Lord values, and the believer who answers the plea is the believer whose help the Lord rewards, and the plea of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the power is the service, and the believer who reads the plea is the believer whose service the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the duty of the strong: the people pleaded and the king answered, and the believer who is able is the believer whose help the Lord blesses, and the plea is the teaching that the power is the service of the weak. The verse is the record of the plea, and the teaching of the service that the power enables.",
  "95": "“He responded, “What my Lord has provided for me is far better. But assist me with resources, and I will build a barrier between you and them.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: “What my Lord has provided for me is far better. But assist me with resources, and I will build a barrier between you and them,” or “qala ma makkannee feehi rabbee khayrun fa-a‘eenoonee bi-quwwatin aj‘al baynakum wa baynahum radman,” and the commentators note the reply, for Dhul-Qarnayn refused the tribute, and the refusal is the generosity, the declaration that the provision of the Lord is the better, and the reply then asked the assistance of the resources, and the verse is the record of the reply, the generosity and the plan that the following verses fulfill. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the refusal of the tribute is the gratitude, and the request of the resources is the plan, and the verse is the record of the reply, the generosity of the king.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the generosity, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own detachment. The believer is taught that the provision of the Lord is the better, and the one who is given the power is the one whose detachment the Lord values, and the believer who serves without the price is the believer whose service the Lord rewards, and the reply of Dhul-Qarnayn is the model, the refusal of the tribute and the request of the help, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose detachment the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the detachment of the powerful: the king refused the tribute, and the believer who serves without the price is the believer whose service the Lord blesses, and the reply is the teaching that the provision of the Lord is the better. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the detachment that the power requires.",
  "96": "““Bring me blocks of iron!” Then, when he had filled up ˹the gap˺ between the two mountains, he ordered, “Blow!” When the iron became red hot, he said, “Bring me molten copper to pour over it.”” records the building, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the building: “Bring me blocks of iron! Then, when he had filled up the gap between the two mountains, he ordered, ‘Blow!’ When the iron became red hot, he said, ‘Bring me molten copper to pour over it,’” or “atoonee zubara al-hadeedi hatta itha sawa bayna as-sadafayni qala unfukhoo hatta itha ja‘alahu naran qala atoonee ufrigh ‘alayhi qitran,” and the commentators note the building, for Dhul-Qarnayn built the barrier with the iron and the copper, and the building is the skill, the engineering that the Lord had granted, and the verse is the record of the building, the barrier that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the iron and the copper are the two materials, and the blowing and the pouring are the two stages, and the verse is the record of the building, the barrier that the king raised. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the iron blocks are the foundation, and the molten copper is the seal, and the verse is the record of the building, the barrier that the king constructed.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the building, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own work. The believer is taught that the work of the protection is the duty, and the one who builds the barrier is the one whose work the Lord values, and the believer who uses the means for the good is the believer whose work the Lord blesses, and the building of Dhul-Qarnayn is the model, the barrier of the iron and the copper, and the believer who reads the building is the believer whose work the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the work of the protection: the king built the barrier, and the believer who works for the good is the believer whose work the Lord rewards, and the building is the teaching that the means are the instruments of the service. The verse is the record of the building, and the teaching of the work that the protection requires.",
  "97": "“And so the enemies could neither scale nor tunnel through it.”” records the result, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the result: “And so the enemies could neither scale nor tunnel through it,” or “fa-ma ista‘oo an yazharoohu wa ma istata‘oo lahu naqban,” and the commentators note the result, for the enemies could not scale the barrier and could not tunnel through it, and the verse is the record of the result, the success of the barrier, and the commentators note the significance, for the two negations are the completeness, the declaration that the barrier sealed the pass, and the verse is the record of the result, the barrier that the following verse declares the mercy. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the scaling is the climbing, and the tunnelling is the piercing, and the verse is the record of the result, the barrier that the enemies could not breach.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the result, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the work of the protection is the successful, and the one who builds for the good is the one whose work the Lord completes, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the work is the believer whose work the Lord crowns, and the result of the barrier is the teaching, the declaration that the service of the strong is the shield of the weak, and the believer who reads the result is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the success of the service: the barrier held, and the believer who works for the good is the believer whose work the Lord completes, and the result is the teaching that the power is the shield of the weak. The verse is the record of the result, and the teaching of the success that the service attains.",
  "98": "“He declared, “This is a mercy from my Lord. But when the promise of my Lord comes to pass, He will level it to the ground. And my Lord’s promise is ever true.”” records the declaration, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the declaration: “This is a mercy from my Lord. But when the promise of my Lord comes to pass, He will level it to the ground. And my Lord’s promise is ever true,” or “qala hatha rahmatun min rabbee fa-itha ja’a wa‘du rabbee ja‘alahu dakka’a wa kana wa‘du rabbee haqqan,” and the commentators note the declaration, for Dhul-Qarnayn attributed the barrier to the mercy of the Lord, and the declaration is the humility, the acknowledgment that the barrier is the gift, and the warning that the barrier would be levelled at the promise, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the humility and the certainty that the story closes with, and the commentators note the significance, for the attribution of the mercy is the humility of the powerful, and the warning of the levelling is the certainty of the end, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the mercy is the gift, and the levelling is the end, and the verse is the record of the completion, the humility and the certainty of the king.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the humility, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own attribution. The believer is taught that the work is the mercy, and the one who attributes the success to the Lord is the one whose humility the Lord values, and the believer who knows the end of the works is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the declaration of Dhul-Qarnayn is the model, the humility of the powerful, and the believer who reads the declaration is the believer whose humility the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humility of the success: the king attributed the mercy, and the believer who attributes the success to the Lord is the believer whose humility the Lord accepts, and the warning of the levelling is the teaching that the works of the world are the temporary. The verse is the record of the declaration, and the teaching of the humility that the power requires.",
  "99": "\"On that Day, We will let them surge like waves over one another. Later, the Trumpet will be blown, and We will gather all people together\" shifts from Zul-Qarnain's time to the Last Day. The previous verse said the wall will be leveled when God's promise comes. This verse describes what happens after.\n\n\"On that Day\" refers to the time when the barrier is brought down and Gog and Magog are released. They will surge like waves, spreading across the earth in vast numbers, overwhelming everything in their path. The image of waves suggests an unstoppable multitude, crashing over one another in their rush forward.\n\nThis is one of the major signs of the Day of Judgment. Authentic hadiths describe Gog and Magog as vast numbers of people who will spread corruption, drink bodies of water dry, and besiege the believers and the prophet Jesus, who will return to defeat them. God will eventually destroy them through a disease or worms that strike their necks, according to the hadiths.\n\nAfter these events, the Trumpet will be blown. This is the trumpet of resurrection, blown by the angel Israfil. At its sound, everything in the heavens and earth will faint, except those God wills. Then it will be blown again, and all people will stand resurrected before the Lord of all being.\n\nThe phrase \"We will gather all people together\" means no one will be left out. Every human being who ever lived will be assembled for judgment. The vast hordes of Gog and Magog, the people they oppressed, Zul-Qarnain and his generation, and every soul before and after, all will be gathered.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder that history has an end. The surging of nations, the chaos of the final age, and the trumpet of resurrection are all certain. The walls we build, however strong, will fall. The only lasting protection is faith and righteous deeds, which endure beyond the trumpet and into the Hereafter.",
  "100": "\"On that Day We will display Hell clearly for the disbelievers\" describes what happens after the gathering. The previous verse said all people will be gathered. This verse says that on that same Day, Hell will be shown openly to those who disbelieved.\n\nThe word \"display\" means to present, show, or expose. Hell will not be hidden or distant. It will be brought into view for all to see, specifically for the disbelievers to see what they have earned. The Quran elsewhere says that Hell will be brought near on that Day.\n\nThe phrase \"clearly\" means without ambiguity. The disbelievers will see it with their own eyes and know with certainty that it is real and that it is their destination. In this life, they mocked the idea of Hell. On that Day, there will be no mockery.\n\nShowing Hell before the judgment creates a moment of terrifying recognition. Before even being sentenced, the disbelievers see where they are going. There is no more denial, no more bargaining, no more argument. The reality they rejected is in front of them.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning. The Hell that is discussed in the Quran, that some people treat as a metaphor or a distant tale, will one day be displayed clearly. The time to believe in it and seek protection from it is now, while the veil of this world still obscures it.\n\nThe verse also sets up the description of the disbelievers in the next verse, those who were blind to God's reminder in this life. Their blindness then leads to the clear sight of Hell now, a sight that comes too late.\n\n'Displayed clearly' reverses the disbelievers' own policy. The next verse describes them as having 'turned a blind eye to My Reminder,' and their treatment on that Day matches the faculty they chose: they refused to see, and in the end they are made to see, with nothing left between them and the sight.",
  "101": "\"those who turned a blind eye to My Reminder and could not stand listening to it\" describes the spiritual condition of the disbelievers for whom Hell was displayed in the previous verse. The verse does not merely label them as disbelievers. It explains what they did with the reminder when it came to them.\n\nThe phrase \"turned a blind eye\" translates an Arabic word that means to place a cover or a curtain over something. They deliberately covered their sight from God's Reminder. The Reminder, or \"dhikr,\" refers to the Quran and the prophetic teachings. It is called a reminder because it brings back to mind the truths that human beings already know deep down: that God is one, that they are accountable, and that righteousness matters.\n\nThese people did not merely fail to understand. They chose not to look. They placed a veil between themselves and the message. This is a description of active rejection rather than innocent ignorance.\n\nThe second phrase, \"could not stand listening to it,\" deepens the picture. They could not bear to hear the Quran. Its words of judgment, its demands for justice, and its call to worship one God made them uncomfortable. They preferred poetry, entertainment, and the talk of the marketplace because those things did not challenge their way of life.\n\nThis is a common spiritual condition. When truth is uncomfortable, people do not merely disagree with it. They avoid it. They change the subject, mock the speaker, or fill their ears with other noise. The Quran says this is not a sign of weak evidence. It is a sign of a heart that has chosen blindness.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a personal test. How do you respond when the Quran confronts something you love or defend? Do you listen carefully, even when it is uncomfortable? Or do you turn away, look for distractions, and find reasons not to hear? The answer reveals whether you are among those who turn a blind eye to the Reminder or among those who hold fast to it.",
  "102": "\"Do the disbelievers think they can simply take My servants as lords instead of Me? We have surely prepared Hell as an accommodation for the disbelievers\" refutes the false hopes of those who worshipped created beings besides God. The previous verses described the disbelievers as blind to the Reminder. This verse exposes the futility of their substitutes for God.\n\nThe phrase \"take My servants as lords\" includes every form of devotion given to something created. It applies to those who worshipped angels, saints, idols, jinn, human leaders, or their own desires. A \"lord\" is whoever or whatever is given ultimate authority, obedience, fear, or hope. When a person follows a created being against God's commands, they have taken that being as a lord in some sense.\n\nThe verse points out that these so-called lords are themselves God's servants. They have no power to save, protect, or intercede without God's permission. Even the angels and the prophets cannot be worshipped, and they will dissociate themselves from those who gave them divine status. The Quran tells of Jesus, on the Day of Judgment, denying that he ever asked people to worship him.\n\n\"Do the disbelievers think they can simply...?\" is a rhetorical question exposing the foolishness of their choice. They have chosen protectors who cannot protect even themselves. The idols cannot defend their worshippers, and the human leaders whom people obey against God will not take responsibility for them on the Last Day.\n\nThe phrase \"We have surely prepared Hell as an accommodation\" uses striking language. An \"accommodation\" is usually a place where a guest is welcomed and made to rest. Here it is used ironically: the disbelievers' chosen hospitality is Hell. It is the home they have prepared for themselves through their disbelief.\n\nFor the reader, the verse asks whom you are taking as a lord besides God. Is there a created authority that you obey when it contradicts God? Is there a desire, a person, or an institution that holds the place of ultimate loyalty in your life? If so, examine that loyalty now, before the Day when no servant can help another.",
  "103": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'Shall we inform you of who will lose the most deeds?'\" introduces one of the most sobering descriptions in the entire Quran. The previous verse said the disbelievers have Hell as their accommodation. This verse now identifies the people whose loss is the greatest.\n\nThe question \"Shall we inform you?\" is meant to grab attention. The listener is about to hear something they may not expect. The greatest losers are not only the obvious disbelievers. They include people whose deeds look impressive on the outside but whose inner orientation is wrong.\n\nThe phrase \"the most deeds\" suggests people who do many things, people who are busy, active, and even religiously observant. They pray, they fast, they give, they fight, they work hard. But their deeds are lost because they lack the foundation that makes deeds acceptable.\n\nA hadith helps explain this. The Prophet said that on the Day of Judgment, a man will be brought who was killed in the cause of God, or who gave large amounts of charity, or who recited the Quran extensively. When asked why he did these things, he will admit that he did them so people would say he was brave, or generous, or knowledgeable. The verdict will be that he received exactly what he sought in the world, the praise of people, and he will have no reward in the Hereafter.\n\nThe verse is not saying that good deeds are worthless. It is saying that deeds without sincere faith and correct orientation to God are lost. A tree without roots may look full of leaves, but it will topple in the storm.\n\nFor the reader, this verse is a call to examine intentions. Why do you do what you do? Are your deeds done for God alone, or for the praise of people? The person whose deeds are lost in the Hereafter is often the one who looked most successful in this world. True loss is not a small amount of bad deeds. True loss is a large amount of deeds that come to nothing.",
  "104": "\"They are those whose efforts are in vain in this worldly life, while they think they are doing good!\" identifies exactly who the greatest losers are. The previous verse asked the question. This verse gives the answer.\n\nThe phrase \"efforts are in vain\" means their works are nullified, wasted, and brought to nothing. They spend their lives striving, laboring, and sacrificing, but none of it carries weight with God. The Arabic word for vain suggests something that collapses, like a structure whose foundation has crumbled.\n\nThe key phrase is \"in this worldly life.\" Their deeds belong entirely to this world. They may be morally good in a worldly sense: feeding the hungry, building hospitals, writing books, leading movements. But if these deeds are not rooted in faith in God and oriented toward the Hereafter, they remain worldly deeds with a worldly reward, and there is nothing for them in the next life.\n\nThe second half of the verse is devastating: \"while they think they are doing good.\" They are not hypocrites in the simple sense. They genuinely believe they are doing well. They are pleased with their work, admired by their followers, and confident in their own goodness. But sincerity without guidance, and good deeds without faith, are not enough.\n\nThis is why Islam ties action to knowledge. A person may be sincere, but if they are sincerely wrong, their sincerity does not guide them. A traveler who sincerely drives in the wrong direction does not reach their destination simply because they are earnest. The Quran calls people to both sincerity and truth.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning against self-satisfaction. The fact that you think you are doing good is not enough. You must measure your deeds against the revelation of God, ask whether your intentions are pure, and check whether your orientation is toward the Hereafter. The person who assumes they are fine without examining themselves may discover that their efforts belong entirely to this world.",
  "105": "\"It is they who reject the signs of their Lord and their meeting with Him, rendering their deeds void, so We will not give their deeds any weight on Judgment Day\" explains why the efforts of those people came to nothing. The previous verse described them as thinking they are doing good. This verse identifies the root of their loss.\n\nTwo things are rejected: the signs of their Lord, and their meeting with Him. The signs include the Quran, the prophets, the evidence in creation, and the moral law written in the human heart. The meeting with God refers to the Day of Judgment, resurrection, and accountability.\n\nWhen a person rejects these two things, their deeds are rendered void. This does not mean that God is unjust. It means that deeds depend on faith for their eternal value. A good action done without faith is not necessarily a crime in this world, and may even benefit others, but it does not qualify as worship of God or earn a reward in the Hereafter.\n\nThe phrase \"We will not give their deeds any weight\" uses the imagery of the scale on the Day of Judgment. Deeds are weighed, and even a small good deed may tip the scale. But for these people, their many deeds weigh nothing because they lack the foundation of faith. A massive pile of cotton may be large, but it has little weight compared to a small nugget of gold.\n\nThis is consistent with the entire Quran's teaching that faith is the root. Good deeds are the branches, but without the root, the branches die. The Quran says that God does not wrong people, but people wrong themselves.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder that no amount of good work can replace faith. Build on the foundation of belief in God and the Hereafter, and then fill your life with good deeds. The deeds may be small, but if they rest on true faith, they will have weight on the Day when nothing else matters.",
  "106": "\"That is their reward: Hell, for their disbelief and mockery of My signs and messengers\" gives the final verdict on the people described in the previous verses. The previous verse said their deeds have no weight. This verse says what their actual reward is.\n\nThe word \"reward\" is used with bitter irony. The reward they earned for their disbelief is Hell. The word in Arabic, \"jaza,\" simply means what comes back to you in return for what you did. Their deeds return to them as the Fire.\n\nThree reasons are given. The first is disbelief. They rejected the truth after it came to them. The second is mockery of God's signs. They did not merely ignore the Quran and the prophets. They laughed at them, ridiculed them, and treated them as jokes. The third is mockery of the messengers, who were mocked in every age.\n\nMockery is a particularly dangerous spiritual condition because it seals the heart. A person who sincerely disagrees can still reconsider. A person who mocks has stopped treating the truth seriously. They have replaced inquiry with contempt, and contempt is difficult to reverse.\n\nThe Quran repeatedly describes how earlier peoples mocked their messengers and were destroyed. The people of Noah mocked the ark. The people of Hud mocked the warning. The Quraysh mocked Muhammad. Mockery gives a temporary feeling of superiority, but it ends in humiliation.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a warning never to treat the things of God as jokes. This includes the Quran, the Prophet, the rituals of worship, and the teachings of the religion. A Muslim may struggle to practice, but they should never join those who mock. A lighthearted attitude toward sacred things can be a sign that the heart has hardened.\n\nThe order of the three reasons follows a progression. Disbelief is the inner position, mockery of the signs is the public posture toward revelation, and mockery of the messengers is the treatment of the people who carried it. Thought became speech, and speech became behaviour toward real human beings, which is the route every settled hostility travels.",
  "107": "\"Indeed, those who believe and do good will have the Gardens of Paradise as an accommodation\" contrasts the destination of the believers with the reward of the disbelievers. The previous verse said the disbelievers have Hell. This verse says the believers have the Gardens of Paradise.\n\nThe structure is deliberately parallel. The disbelievers have Hell as an \"accommodation.\" The believers have the Gardens of Paradise as an \"accommodation.\" The same word is used to emphasize that every person will reside in the home they have prepared for themselves.\n\nThe \"Gardens of Paradise,\" or \"Jannat al-Firdaws,\" refers to the highest part of Paradise, the central and most noble garden. The Prophet taught that when you ask God for Paradise, ask for al-Firdaws, because it is the highest part, above it is the Throne of the Most Merciful, and from it flow the rivers of Paradise.\n\nThe two conditions are the same formula repeated throughout the Quran: \"those who believe and do good.\" Belief alone is not sufficient if it produces no action, and good deeds alone are not sufficient without belief. The two must come together, like a bird needs two wings.\n\nThe word \"accommodation\" is meaningful. Paradise is not a temporary hotel where you stay for a few nights. It is an eternal home, a place of rest, hospitality, and belonging. The believers are received as honored guests who will never be asked to leave.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a source of hope and motivation. Whatever difficulties you face in this life, there is a home prepared for those who believe and do good. The contrast between the two accommodations could not be sharper. Choose the deeds that lead to the Gardens, and avoid the path that leads to the Fire.\n\nThe pairing of the two accommodations also completes the surah's architecture. It opened with a righteous heir granted to a praying old man, passed through those who inherited Scripture and those who earned the Fire, and closes by housing everyone according to what they did with what they were given. The chapter about inheritance ends with the only inheritances that are permanent.",
  "108": "\"where they will be forever, never desiring anywhere else\" describes the contentment of the people of Paradise. The previous verse said they will have the Gardens. This verse says they will remain there and will never want to leave.\n\nThe word \"forever\" establishes the eternity of Paradise. There is no end to it, no aging, no boredom, and no expulsion. The believers will be in a state of unending bliss. This is the fulfillment of every human longing for permanence, a longing that nothing in this world can satisfy.\n\nThe phrase \"never desiring anywhere else\" is striking. Even if offered a choice, they would not want to leave Paradise. They are completely satisfied with where they are. There is no restlessness, no \"what if,\" no grass-is-greener thinking. Their home is perfect, and they know it.\n\nThis contrasts sharply with the nature of worldly life. In this world, people always want more. A person buys a house and wants a bigger one. They reach a position and want a higher one. They travel and want to go somewhere else. This restlessness is part of human nature in this world, because this world was not built to fully satisfy.\n\nParadise is different. It is the place where every desire finds its proper object and every longing is finally at rest. The Quran says that in Paradise, the believers will have whatever their souls desire and whatever delights their eyes.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a reminder that your restlessness in this world is not a defect. It is a sign that you were created for something more. Do not try to satisfy your deepest longings with the things of this world, because they were designed to point beyond themselves. Use this world as a path to the home where you will never want to leave.",
  "109": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'If the ocean were ink for writing the Words of my Lord, it would certainly run out before the Words of my Lord were finished, even if We refilled it with its equal'\" uses a powerful image to describe the infinite knowledge and words of God. The previous verses described the rewards of the Hereafter. This verse explains that no amount of created material can contain God's knowledge.\n\nThe image is vivid. Imagine the entire ocean as ink. Imagine using that ink to write down the wisdom, commands, decrees, and knowledge of God. The ocean would be exhausted before the words of God would be exhausted. Even if another ocean, equal to the first, were brought as a refill, it too would run out.\n\nThe phrase \"Words of my Lord\" refers to everything God knows, decrees, creates, and says. It includes the Quran, but it is much broader than the Quran. It includes the divine words that sustain the universe, that govern every event, and that record every deed. These words are infinite because God's knowledge is infinite.\n\nThe verse teaches two important truths. First, God's knowledge is limitless. Human knowledge is always partial, always growing, and always subject to correction. God's knowledge is complete and unchanging. Second, God's speech is real and eternal. He speaks, and His words are not created sounds that begin and end. They are an expression of His infinite being.\n\nThis verse also has practical implications. No matter how much you learn, there is always more to learn about God, His revelation, and His creation. The ocean of divine wisdom is never exhausted. A person who thinks they know everything has not yet understood the first thing about God.\n\nFor the reader, the verse invites humility and wonder. The same God whose words cannot be contained by an ocean of ink has spoken to you in the Quran. He has told you what you need to know for your salvation. Receive His words with gratitude, and never tire of learning more.",
  "110": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'I am only a man like you, but it has been revealed to me that your God is only One God. So whoever hopes for the meeting with their Lord, let them do good deeds and associate none in the worship of their Lord'\" is the final verse of Surah al-Kahf. It brings together the themes of the entire chapter into a single, comprehensive statement.\n\nThe Prophet is told to declare that he is only a man. This is the same affirmation made in Surah al-Isra. The messenger is not divine, not an angel, and not a superhuman being. He eats, walks in the markets, and experiences joy and sorrow. What distinguishes him is not his nature, but the revelation he receives.\n\nThe content of that revelation is summarized in a single sentence: \"your God is only One God.\" This is the essence of Islam, the message of every prophet, and the foundation of right action. Tawhid, the oneness of God, is the core truth that the entire chapter has defended through its stories and arguments.\n\nThen comes the practical conclusion. Whoever hopes for the meeting with their Lord should do two things. First, do good deeds. Second, associate none in the worship of their Lord. These two, good deeds and pure monotheism, summarize the life of a believer.\n\nThe phrase \"hopes for the meeting with their Lord\" is significant. It is not \"knows with certainty\" or \"takes for granted.\" It is \"hopes.\" This is the balanced posture of the believer: they work righteousness, they avoid associating partners with God, and they hope in His mercy. They do not presume that their deeds guarantee Paradise, and they do not despair of God's forgiveness.\n\nThe word \"associate none\" includes both obvious and hidden forms of shirk. It includes worshipping idols, but it also includes doing deeds for show, obeying created authorities against God, and loving something as much as God should be loved. The verse that warns against lost deeds earlier in the chapter reinforces this: deeds must be done for God alone.\n\nFor the reader, this final verse is a summary of the entire Islamic life. You are a human being, following a human messenger, who was given a divine message. That message is that God is one. Your response is to believe, do good, purify your worship, and hope for the meeting with your Lord. That is the path of the people of the cave, the two gardens' poor companion, Moses with his humility, and Zul-Qarnain with his justice. It is the path that leads to the Gardens of Paradise."
};
